WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.579 --> 00:00:10.720
&gt;&gt;David Prior: So welcome everybody to the public&nbsp;
board meeting of NHS England and NHS Improvement.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:18.000
We've excluded members of the public because of&nbsp;
the advice on COVID-19.&nbsp; I've got apologies for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.400
absence from Susan Kilsby.&nbsp; Are there any other&nbsp;
declarations of interest anyone would like above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:31.600
and beyond what has already been declared?&nbsp; No,&nbsp;
thank you.&nbsp; And can I approve the minutes of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:38.160
last board meeting, which took place on the&nbsp;
30th of September, please?&nbsp; Good.&nbsp; Thank you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:45.040
all very much.&nbsp; Amanda, I can't actually see&nbsp;
you at the moment, but I think you are there.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:01:02.480
Amanda?&nbsp; Mark, are you there?
&gt;&gt;Mark Cubbon: I am indeed, Chair.&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.600
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I – if – we're&nbsp;
just looking for Amanda.&nbsp; I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.880
thought she – I was told she was in her&nbsp;
office, so she can't be far away.&nbsp; But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:16.960
whether you'd like to extemporise a little bit&nbsp;
on the operations over the last couple of months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.880
while we're waiting for Amanda.
&gt;&gt;Mark Cubbon: Of course, very happy to, Chair.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:30.320
So I'll just remind everyone that essentially we&nbsp;
are still running the whole of the NHS on – as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:36.880
level-three instance, and that is a regionally-led&nbsp;
response and coordination, but we do have a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:44.480
clear national oversight.&nbsp; The prevalence of COVID&nbsp;
in hospitals remains significant.&nbsp; It does present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:50.160
a challenge into terms of day-to-day operations&nbsp;
of the service.&nbsp; And we have been running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:57.680
at about 7,000 patients at any one point in time&nbsp;
throughout November, although just recently it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.840
fallen back slightly.&nbsp; But nonetheless with that&nbsp;
number of beds occupied with patients who need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.760
our care and some of the challenges around&nbsp;
ensuring that we have very strict infection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:14.720
prevention and control measures in place, it&nbsp;
has generated quite a lot of pressure from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:22.400
a bed-occupancy perspective almost in all parts of&nbsp;
the country.&nbsp; Pauline will talk in more detail -&nbsp;

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.880
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Mark.&nbsp; Thank you&nbsp;
very – Mark, thanks very much.&nbsp; Sorry to cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.246
you off now.&nbsp; We're going to come back to you in&nbsp;
a minute.&nbsp; I see that Amanda is on the call now.&nbsp;

00:02:28.246 --> 00:02:29.680
&gt;&gt;Mark Cubbon: Great.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: So thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:35.840
very much for filling in without much prior&nbsp;
warning there.&nbsp; But – so over to you, Amanda.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.565
Huh, right, this is all going -
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you.&nbsp;

00:02:42.565 --> 00:02:44.640
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Oh, here she&nbsp;
is.&nbsp; Excellent.&nbsp; Amanda.&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:53.440
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: So thank you and apologies&nbsp;
for being a minute late.&nbsp; So thank you, David.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.880
Can I start – I know you've given me the&nbsp;
opportunity just to say a few words at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.720
beginning of the meeting.&nbsp; So I'd like to start,&nbsp;
if I may as we usually do, and I know I speak for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:07.200
the whole board when I say this, but to&nbsp;
start by thanking staff across the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.760
NHS for their continued efforts to deal with&nbsp;
the current challenges the service is facing.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:18.240
And working so hard to help patients get the&nbsp;
safe and effective care they need now.&nbsp; And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.480
of course, also to continue to build,&nbsp;
transform and improve for the future.&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:29.920
I'm very pleased that I can say since we last met,&nbsp;
our lifesaving vaccination programme has expanded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:35.360
significantly in response to booster doses&nbsp;
being authorised.&nbsp; So we have now delivered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:41.280
over 95&nbsp;million lifesaving jabs, including&nbsp;
over 30&nbsp;million boosters and third doses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:47.040
which means that already we are able to&nbsp;
see that we are offering very effective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:53.920
extra protection to more than now seven out of ten&nbsp;
people aged 50 and over who are eligible for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.560
booster vaccination.&nbsp; And importantly, we've&nbsp;
also seen hundreds of thousands, more people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.200
coming forward for their first and second doses&nbsp;
over that time too, which I think really does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.240
show the importance of maintaining our evergreen&nbsp;
offer.&nbsp; And I'm sure the board would join me in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:14.000
congratulating the whole of the vaccine deployment&nbsp;
programme at NHSEI but of course, also those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.680
delivering and supporting the programme across the&nbsp;
country for this really remarkable achievement.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:28.320
And that work is vital because there remain even&nbsp;
today over 6,000 patients in hospital with COVID.&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.960
So for this, but also for other reasons,&nbsp;
pressures I think as everybody knows on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:40.080
all parts of the system are intense and&nbsp;
that is particularly being felt in urgent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.120
and emergency care. &nbsp;The measures that we outlined&nbsp;
in the urgent emergency care recovery plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.120
are having an impact.&nbsp; And of course,&nbsp;
since our last meeting, we've also made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:56.720
the first ever specific financial support&nbsp;
fund available for primary care to support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:01.120
access and the availability of appointments&nbsp;
over the coming months.&nbsp; So that's 250&nbsp;million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:07.200
winter access fund.&nbsp; But saying all of that, I&nbsp;
think realistically, the likelihood remains of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:14.800
this being one of, if not the, toughest winters&nbsp;
the NHS has faced.&nbsp; So it is vital that in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:19.760
context we continue to make progress on not just&nbsp;
all of those areas that are outlined in the urgent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:25.200
emergency care plan, but also some of the joint&nbsp;
working with social care and local authorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.720
to help people stay well at home, to be&nbsp;
able to get the care they need in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:32.880
community when they need it and of course, to be&nbsp;
supported to leave hospital as soon as possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:38.960
when they're medically ready to be discharged.
And just as an example of this earlier this week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:44.480
I was up in Leicestershire speaking to their Home&nbsp;
First Team who've brought NHS and social care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:50.080
staff together within their own urgent Community&nbsp;
Response Team to provide a two-hour response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:55.760
to many patients who would otherwise potentially&nbsp;
need a category three or four ambulance response.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:02.160
I mean, it was so impressive.&nbsp; And it's really&nbsp;
clear, I think, that teams like this can play a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:07.600
really valuable role this winter, particularly&nbsp;
with the pressures being reported by care homes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:13.840
and home care providers.&nbsp; So there is some focus&nbsp;
work going on over this winter to ensure we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.640
using the combined skills and expertise of&nbsp;
the NHS and social care professionals.&nbsp; And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:25.120
this model brings that together to take as much&nbsp;
pressure as possible off our ambulance services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:28.880
and other parts of the system this winter.&nbsp; I know&nbsp;
that's something we're going to talk more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:33.520
during the main board meeting.
But implementing urgent Community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.440
Response Teams, of course, was one&nbsp;
of our long-term plan commitments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:44.000
and we have made progress on other commitments too&nbsp;
since our last meeting.&nbsp; Last week, we announced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.160
a national network of 15 specialist clinics&nbsp;
for children living with obesity, children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:53.520
actually, sorry, and young people living with&nbsp;
obesity and other mental and physical problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.400
associated with that.&nbsp; We've also announced&nbsp;
a deal with suppliers that allows us to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:04.080
blood thinning drugs available to up to 610,000&nbsp;
additional patients over the next three years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:10.240
with the potential to prevent more than 20,000&nbsp;
strokes and 5,000 deaths.&nbsp; On cardiovascular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:14.240
disease, we also announced earlier in the month&nbsp;
that hundreds of thousands of people will be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.960
to get at-home blood pressure monitors to allow&nbsp;
remote monitoring of their condition.&nbsp; And what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:23.440
that means is, of course, clinicians can then&nbsp;
act earlier when readings suggest a patient's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:28.320
blood pressure is dangerously high, and the&nbsp;
estimation is that could prevent an additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:33.760
2,200 heart attacks and almost&nbsp;
3,300 strokes over five years.&nbsp;

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:40.160
We continue to work hard, and thanks again to&nbsp;
all of those colleagues involved in this work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:46.000
to get deals in place, to make life changing&nbsp;
new treatments available to NHS patients at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.200
fair prices for taxpayers through our specialised&nbsp;
commissioning and commercial medicines teams.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:55.600
So I know we talked about this before, but just&nbsp;
worth remembering, we've also just in the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:59.680
couple of months been able to make available the&nbsp;
first new treatment for sickle cell disease in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:05.280
over 20 years, as well as the third treatment for&nbsp;
spinal muscular atrophy in less than three years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:10.720
both of which will make a huge impact on the lives&nbsp;
of those who will benefit from them.&nbsp; So really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:15.520
important not to lose sight, I think, of some&nbsp;
of the ways in which despite those pressures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:20.960
we are absolutely able to continue to invest&nbsp;
in the sort of transformational change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:27.680
will really make a difference for our patients.
Just a word, if I may, on ICSs because colleagues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:32.400
will have noticed that this week, the Health and&nbsp;
Care Bill has been passed in the House of Commons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:36.240
so it will now continue its journey through&nbsp;
the House of Lords beginning next month.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:41.360
Our preparations for the bill's passage continue&nbsp;
with the vast majority now of ICSs having in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:47.920
place a designate chair and a designate chief&nbsp;
executive.&nbsp; The quality of those appointments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:52.960
I think for both types of roles can really give&nbsp;
us a great deal of confidence that systems are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:57.040
in good hands going forward and are ready to&nbsp;
be, and I've seen this myself from the visits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:01.920
I've done recently and conversations I've had,&nbsp;
that they really are ready to be ambitious about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.400
working in partnership to achieve better health&nbsp;
and better care for the communities they serve.&nbsp;

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:13.200
To further assist them and their teams,&nbsp;
again, just to note, since we last met,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:18.880
I have asked Claire Fuller, who is the ICS leader&nbsp;
in Surrey Heartlands to lead a rapid piece of work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:24.720
to give practical advice on how primary care&nbsp;
networks can take their place within ICSs as,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:30.240
of course, again, envisaged in the long-term&nbsp;
plan.&nbsp; Her work is going to focus on how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:34.720
we best ensure that we're forging those&nbsp;
partnerships between primary, community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:39.200
mental health services, and other partners&nbsp;
to really accelerate the preventative and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.600
out-of-hospital offer for patients in every&nbsp;
part of the country from April onwards.&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:48.720
For those people who need elective care,&nbsp;
we've been working with partners on our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:52.800
elective recovery strategy, and that's going to&nbsp;
be published soon, and it will set out the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:57.760
we're doing to support elective care and reduce&nbsp;
waits for patients.&nbsp; In the meantime, and again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:01.760
I know we'll talk about this more during the&nbsp;
meeting, whilst the size of the waiting list has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:07.600
continued to grow, we are seeing excellent work&nbsp;
across the country to increase activity levels.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:14.160
And the strategy of course will be about both how&nbsp;
we safeguard elective activity over what I've just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.640
said and we all know will be a very busy winter,&nbsp;
but also how we then build on that momentum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:22.800
over the coming years.
Nationally, and this is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:30.640
again, hugely important to us both now and in the&nbsp;
future, but the government has confirmed this week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:35.040
that NHS England and NHS Improvement will&nbsp;
be assuming additional responsibilities in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:41.200
supporting the health service in some key priority&nbsp;
areas, and those are workforce and digital.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:45.840
And we've always said, I've said, we know,&nbsp;
and I know colleagues agree with me on this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:53.120
the workforce and digital need to be, should be&nbsp;
absolutely at the heart of everything we do in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:59.680
NHS.&nbsp; So I think this is a really important&nbsp;
moment for us.&nbsp; So the two announcements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:04.640
Health Education England will be merging with&nbsp;
us with a working assumption at the moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:08.640
but obviously subject to further discussion and&nbsp;
agreement, that it would at least initially be as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:15.920
a distinct directorate.&nbsp; And then NHS Digital&nbsp;
and NHSX will also be coming in, merging into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:20.640
our organisation following Laura's review with&nbsp;
the former, again, initially as a new director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:26.720
led by Simon Bolton as the CIO, and the latter&nbsp;
integrating into the Transformation Directorate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:32.160
both of course, under Tim's leadership.
And all three of those organisations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:37.760
have played an absolutely vital role during the&nbsp;
last two years.&nbsp; And of course, before that as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:43.440
well.&nbsp; But I think we all feel that these changes&nbsp;
represent a real opportunity for the NHS to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:48.720
on that progress and approach workforce planning&nbsp;
and digital transformation in an ever more joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:53.920
up and coherent way.&nbsp; And it really does give&nbsp;
us that chance to make real what we say and put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.920 --> 00:12:02.160
workforce and digital at the heart of everything&nbsp;
the NHS does.&nbsp; We know though, and we've had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:07.280
a bit of a chance to talk about this though not&nbsp;
much because it's relatively recent announcements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:12.080
that a lot of work will be needed to ensure we&nbsp;
get the transition right as an organisation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:17.280
both for our collective staff and for&nbsp;
the NHS system, which is why I know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:22.720
and I'm sure we will revisit this at future board&nbsp;
meetings, but we're beginning now, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:28.000
just to work together on what that transition&nbsp;
plan will look like over the next few months.&nbsp;

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:36.640
So for me, just the immediate priorities as&nbsp;
we look ahead are responding effectively to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:43.040
pressures over winter, maintaining our life-saving&nbsp;
vaccination programme, continuing the recovery of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:48.240
crucial non-COVID services, including, of course,&nbsp;
areas like maternity and mental health as we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:53.360
discussing today, and obviously, tackling the&nbsp;
elective care backlog too.&nbsp; But fourthly and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:59.280
crucially, to keep transforming the NHS.&nbsp; To keep&nbsp;
building on the beneficial changes we've seen over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:05.440
the past two years with a renewed focus on&nbsp;
prevention, on partnerships, on harnessing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:11.600
the power of digital and data, and of course,&nbsp;
underpinning all of that on our workforce too.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:17.440
And that is so that as we emerge from the pandemic&nbsp;
we can be as well-placed as we possibly can to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:22.880
face our longer-term challenges and take&nbsp;
those opportunities that we know we have.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:30.240
I've talked about this recently both NHS leaders&nbsp;
and to leaders from local authorities too and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:36.800
I've had a really good response actually from&nbsp;
people which shows me, again, that the ambition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:43.920
across the NHS and from our partners to do&nbsp;
our best for patients and communities remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:49.040
absolutely paramount and remains strong&nbsp;
even during the most pressured times.&nbsp;

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:53.440
So can I finish off my kind of&nbsp;
opening remarks by just saying again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:57.600
thank you to everybody.&nbsp; To all of our NHS&nbsp;
staff, to all of those working with them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:01.840
to meet the challenges of the present as well&nbsp;
as to take the opportunities of the future.&nbsp;

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:08.160
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much&nbsp;
for that.&nbsp; Very comprehensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:11.760
excellent sort of introduction to the&nbsp;
meeting.&nbsp; I wonder whether I could just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:17.600
turn to Steve, our medical director, just as&nbsp;
another, maybe a little bit more of a comment on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:22.720
how the vaccination programme is going, Steve.
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Well I think as Amanda said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:28.960
the vaccination programme is going extremely well&nbsp;
at the moment.&nbsp; NHS staff and volunteers are doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:36.000
a tremendous job in getting those jabs into arms.&nbsp;&nbsp;
The focus at the moment is clearly on the booster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:44.160
doses.&nbsp; This week, we opened up to the 40 to&nbsp;
49-year olds following the advice from JCVI.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:50.400
That meant around 500,000 people who had their&nbsp;
second dose six weeks ago became eligible this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:57.040
week.&nbsp; And another 500,000 in that group were&nbsp;
eligible to make a booking for when they became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:01.600
eligible in six months because we've opened up the&nbsp;
national booking service which means that after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:05.440
five months, from your second dose, you&nbsp;
can book for that six month appointment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:12.400
in advance so that it's in the diary, hopefully&nbsp;
days or on the day that you become eligible.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:22.640
So a lot of people are taking up boosters.&nbsp; At the&nbsp;
moment, I think we are over the 12&nbsp;million mark.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:25.120
I can check the figures for you in a&nbsp;
minute.&nbsp; I will have the latest ones.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:33.120
And more people coming forward all the time.&nbsp;
We're also increasing the number of sites that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:40.720
people can access the booster dose and vaccines&nbsp;
at.&nbsp; Focus on more community pharmacy sites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:45.360
for instance, because we know that ease of&nbsp;
booking and ease of access are the two things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:52.640
make a difference in terms of people coming&nbsp;
forward.&nbsp; So as ever as we go into winter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:58.640
we do see some waning in immunity from the first&nbsp;
two doses, it's really important that those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:05.440
booster doses are not just seen as a top up, but&nbsp;
as a real critical way of maintaining immunity.&nbsp;

00:16:06.320 --> 00:16:13.280
Now, as Amanda said, we have around 6,000 patients&nbsp;
in hospital in England at the moment with COVID,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:18.960
but in the last week or two, we have seen a&nbsp;
drop – a little bit of a drop in admissions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:24.080
and that number.&nbsp; And if you look at&nbsp;
the test and trace data ONS would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:30.720
and do say that one in 65 people in England&nbsp;
have COVID at the moment.&nbsp; But in the published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:35.280
test and trace data, infection rates are rising&nbsp;
again in children, but actually in the over 60s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:40.320
which of course is the highest risk group in&nbsp;
terms of age for hospitalisation, we're seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:44.720
gradual falls.&nbsp; And it may be that that is an&nbsp;
early sign that the boosters are starting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:51.680
play a role in preventing hospitalisation over&nbsp;
the winter.&nbsp; We will see how that turns out as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:56.800
we analyse the data over the next few weeks but it&nbsp;
really emphasises the importance that when you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:02.320
called for that booster dose, to come and get it.
Meanwhile, we continue to roll out in the 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:07.840
to 15-year olds in school and 16 to 17-year&nbsp;
olds.&nbsp; JCVI has changed the recommendations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:12.480
as to when you get the first dose, if&nbsp;
you haven't had it.&nbsp; In that age group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:16.560
if you've already had COVID, it's 12 weeks after&nbsp;
you've had COVID.&nbsp; So that's been a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:19.920
of course correction in how we do that.&nbsp;
And then finally, of course, we are still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:25.280
getting people coming forward for their&nbsp;
first dose.&nbsp; And there's an evergreen offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:32.080
if you haven't had your first dose of vaccine,&nbsp;
get it.&nbsp; It's more important than ever as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:39.360
go into the winter to make sure that people have&nbsp;
levels of immunity against COVID, so the vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:43.520
programme is really key to protecting yourself,&nbsp;
protecting your loved ones and of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.320 --> 00:17:48.560
managing over winter.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:52.480
Steve, thanks very much for that.&nbsp; So&nbsp;
should we move then on to the next report,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:57.920
operations report.&nbsp; And sorry, Mark, you started&nbsp;
and then you stopped.&nbsp; It's your go again, please.&nbsp;

00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:03.120
&gt;&gt;Mark Cubbon: Chair, thank you very&nbsp;
much.&nbsp; So I won't repeat the updates Amanda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:08.080
and Steve have provided, but I'll just&nbsp;
draw out a few things if I could and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:14.240
then lead into an update we're going to hear from&nbsp;
Pauline Philip and from Claire Murdoch later from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:20.800
emergency care, elective care and then onto mental&nbsp;
health.&nbsp; But from a cancer perspective we now have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:28.800
the campaign that we put out which was the 'Help&nbsp;
us to help you' campaign.&nbsp; We've seen thankfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:34.960
a really good response to that with more patients&nbsp;
coming forward with symptoms, possible symptoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:40.480
that is, or perhaps some urgent referrals from&nbsp;
GPs where patients can go and get onto pathways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:45.120
that will either rule out or confirm a&nbsp;
cancer diagnosis and ensure patients have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:49.760
prompt access to treatment.&nbsp; And we're going&nbsp;
to continue to prioritise, of course cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:57.520
patients over the coming weeks and months ahead.&nbsp;
So that's some good news.&nbsp; But that does present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:02.480
a whole heap of challenges as we are trying to&nbsp;
respond to the increased number.&nbsp; We've seen some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:07.520
record levels now of referrals coming through&nbsp;
to our cancer services.&nbsp; That's a good thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:12.320
for patients, but we're quickly ensuring that&nbsp;
we can meet the demand that we are now seeing.&nbsp;

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:19.520
Primary care continues to be really busy.&nbsp; So&nbsp;
if we compare September 2019 to the September&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:26.240
position, just now they're seeing 5.5% more&nbsp;
activities going through primary care.&nbsp; And we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:30.480
know primary care has been doing a fantastic&nbsp;
contribution to the successes that Amanda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:35.600
shared earlier and indeed Steve, in terms&nbsp;
of the vaccination programme.&nbsp; So they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:40.560
making a fantastic contribution and the&nbsp;
service is incredibly busy as a result.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:47.680
From a long-COVID perspective, we now have&nbsp;
the 90 specialist units all up and running,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:56.880
14 of which are dedicated for paediatric units to&nbsp;
support patients in a post-COVID period to ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:03.120
that they have the right diagnostic tests, they&nbsp;
can have the right tailor-made rehabilitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:10.480
to support a recovery as quickly as possible&nbsp;
according to the individual circumstances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:15.040
Chair, I'm going to finish there and&nbsp;
then pass on to Pauline, if I could,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:20.000
who will update us on more detail around&nbsp;
urgent care and in indeed talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:21.237
our elective recovery work.&nbsp; Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Mark.&nbsp; Pauline.&nbsp;

00:20:21.237 --> 00:20:29.040
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Thank you, Mark.&nbsp; And so I'm&nbsp;
just going to say a little bit about winter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:33.840
and the pressures that we're facing from&nbsp;
an elective and from a UEC perspective.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:41.840
The paper sets out the data and basically tells&nbsp;
us where performance is at the moment.&nbsp; For the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:50.720
coming months, from an elective angle our priority&nbsp;
is clearly to treat people who our clinicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:59.280
prioritise, so our most urgent patients.&nbsp; Those&nbsp;
patients waiting for cancer care, et cetera, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:06.800
also patients who've been waiting the longest in&nbsp;
the system.&nbsp; At the same time and alongside that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:14.640
we are very much aware of the pressures that exist&nbsp;
in UEC at the moment.&nbsp; And our priorities there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:22.880
start with looking at our 999 calls ensuring that&nbsp;
those calls can be answered in a timely manner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:27.520
that ambulances can get to people&nbsp;
when they need an ambulance.&nbsp; Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:32.640
within the standards that we have set and in&nbsp;
a timely manner.&nbsp; That when people need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:39.200
admitted to hospital from an emergency department&nbsp;
that that can be done in a short period, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.200 --> 00:21:44.400
also that when people need to be discharged from&nbsp;
hospital, that we can do that in a supportive way.&nbsp;

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:52.080
So in order to deliver that agenda from elective&nbsp;
right through to urgent and emergency care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:58.560
our focus is first of all on acknowledging&nbsp;
the wonderful work that our staff are doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.560 --> 00:22:01.200
but supporting them during this period.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:08.720
Making the working environment and making the&nbsp;
opportunities that they have attractive to them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:14.000
so that they will continue to work with us and&nbsp;
that they won't leave to go and work in other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:19.360
industries, et cetera.&nbsp; Recruiting more staff,&nbsp;
Ruth might want to say a little bit about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.360 --> 00:22:25.040
from a nursing point of view, but I think&nbsp;
all staff groups are actively involved.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:31.840
We've had a lot of investment and basically we&nbsp;
are seeing numbers increase on a daily basis.&nbsp;

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:38.720
The next bit of work that we've had a&nbsp;
lot of focus on is trying to separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:46.400
elective from non-elective care.&nbsp; And again, a&nbsp;
significant investment into that.&nbsp; So that as UEC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:51.760
pressures continue to increase, that we don't&nbsp;
end up having to cancel some of the priority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:58.160
elective work that I referred to earlier.&nbsp;&nbsp;
We're working very closely with IS partners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.160 --> 00:23:05.920
to agree how we can make the best possible use of&nbsp;
joint capacity.&nbsp; And as Amanda referred to in her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:12.560
opening remarks, really focusing on doing things&nbsp;
differently.&nbsp; Implementing new models of care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.560 --> 00:23:17.360
whether it's the work that we're doing on same-day&nbsp;
emergency care, whether it's the work we're doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:24.160
on 111 first, right through to the work that we're&nbsp;
doing on outpatients from an elective perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.160 --> 00:23:31.440
et cetera.&nbsp; And really, really importantly,&nbsp;
working hand in glove with other stakeholders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:38.480
as well as our own staff.&nbsp; And we will set out&nbsp;
the detail of all of this in the elective strategy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.480 --> 00:23:45.120
that will be published in weeks to come and also&nbsp;
in our winter plan.&nbsp; And so I think I will leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:51.040
that summary there, but happy to answer questions.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Pauline.&nbsp; Steve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.040 --> 00:23:53.280
you've got your hand up.
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Yes, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:56.720
I said I would just have a quick check of where&nbsp;
we were on the booster figures and I think we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.720 --> 00:24:02.720
around 13.3&nbsp;million, so over the 13&nbsp;million mark.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much.&nbsp; I wonder Ian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:06.640
whether Ian Dodge, whether you'd like to&nbsp;
just comment on the work you are doing on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.640 --> 00:24:12.080
out-of-hospital care, which is such an important&nbsp;
part of how we manage through this winter.&nbsp; Ian.&nbsp;

00:24:12.080 --> 00:24:19.680
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you, David.&nbsp; Well first of all,&nbsp;
on primary care, as Mark said, general practice is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:26.000
incredibly busy at the moment.&nbsp; And the even later&nbsp;
data than this board paper that came out yesterday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:33.280
showed a further increase in activity&nbsp;
significantly above BAU compared to pre-pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.280 --> 00:24:41.120
and that's excluding the COVID vaccination data.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Amanda mentioned the £250&nbsp;million new winter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:49.600
access fund.&nbsp; We have been pleased by the level of&nbsp;
engagement across primary care and across systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.560 --> 00:24:57.040
in designing solutions to help improve same-day&nbsp;
access.&nbsp; And in relation to community services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.240 --> 00:25:06.720
we had deliberately focused investment in '21/'22&nbsp;
on developing the urgent community response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:14.400
services to help avoid ambulance&nbsp;
conveyances and admissions.&nbsp; And again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:19.360
just as Amanda had described her example,&nbsp;
that is beginning to really take effect.&nbsp;

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:29.600
The biggest new change in the last few months&nbsp;
that we've seen across the NHS is an increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.600 --> 00:25:35.840
set of discharge challenges in relation to social&nbsp;
care.&nbsp; This is particularly true in relation to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:43.520
domiciliary care given the workforce challenges&nbsp;
there.&nbsp; there has been a new government fund to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.520 --> 00:25:49.440
try and support that and we are working with&nbsp;
local systems and with the government to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:55.840
and see what more can be done by better join-up&nbsp;
between the NHS and local authority colleagues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:56.400 --> 00:26:02.880
given the really pressing need to come up with&nbsp;
additional solutions in that particular space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:09.760
And then I'd finally mention, David, I think&nbsp;
there's a lot of really encouraging work going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.760 --> 00:26:15.840
on around the country on the adoption&nbsp;
at scale beyond the COVID virtual wards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:22.480
of hospital at home and virtual ward models,&nbsp;
we are seeing that in particular across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:27.920
Southeast, but there are many other parts of&nbsp;
the country as well.&nbsp; And we are working with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:34.320
a lot of those systems to see what we can do&nbsp;
to develop end-state scaling of those models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:35.040 --> 00:26:39.760
not just for the next couple of years, but&nbsp;
actually what could be done potentially later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.760 --> 00:26:43.280
this winter to help alleviate pressures.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.280 --> 00:26:47.680
Well, thanks very much, Ian.&nbsp; Andrew?
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: Thanks, David.&nbsp; I've got a point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.680 --> 00:26:54.960
for Pauline.&nbsp; So I recently visited Southeast&nbsp;
Coast ambulance and I was really struck by, A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.960 --> 00:27:03.360
the pressures on their 999 service, but I was&nbsp;
taken with the 111 first service because it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:10.240
diverting a massive chunk of activity where&nbsp;
you don't need an ambulance, you just need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:16.080
some support.&nbsp; And I was really struck by the&nbsp;
number of slots that they've got for general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:22.080
practice appointments.&nbsp; And I'm just wondering&nbsp;
whether, if we've rolled this out at scale right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:29.280
across all the seven ambulance authorities?
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:36.000
Yes, Andrew.&nbsp; We've rolled it out right across all&nbsp;
of our 111 providers.&nbsp; Clearly, some of our 111&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:44.400
providers aren't ambulance providers.&nbsp; But I think&nbsp;
to really say that 111, it's taking about 44%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:52.720
additional calls if you compare it to the same&nbsp;
period in 2019, 2020.&nbsp; So I think that shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.720 --> 00:27:57.920
the success of the service.&nbsp; Now we all&nbsp;
acknowledge that 111 can only be as good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.920 --> 00:28:04.560
as the services that it links to but I think a lot&nbsp;
of work has gone into that right across the NHS.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:05.120 --> 00:28:12.080
Clearly, a significant number of people continue&nbsp;
to use 111 online and over the winter period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.080 --> 00:28:18.000
we will encourage people to do so even more&nbsp;
because they can get the exact same service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:22.800
and also be connected to a clinician when&nbsp;
necessary.&nbsp; But yes, this service has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:22.800 --> 00:28:30.240
rolled out nationally and I think it's a good news&nbsp;
story for us, but also a great acknowledgement of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.240 --> 00:28:33.600
the work that the staff in 111 are doing.
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: Thank you.&nbsp;

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:40.560
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Pauline. &nbsp;I wonder at&nbsp;
an earlier meeting, Laura Wade-Gery raised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:45.920
a question about how many people are accessing&nbsp;
111 online as distinct from telephone call.&nbsp; I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:45.920 --> 00:28:50.080
wonder if we could just maybe at the next board it&nbsp;
would be quite interesting just to monitor that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.080 --> 00:28:52.640
to see how that is growing.
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Yes.&nbsp;

00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:58.560
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Pauline.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Look, we recognise the huge pressure the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:58.560 --> 00:29:04.000
service is under at the moment.&nbsp; And we&nbsp;
have Aidan Fowler joining us later on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:08.080
I think on a different subject.&nbsp; We might&nbsp;
just sort of ask him to comment on the sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.080 --> 00:29:13.680
of safety implications of that later in&nbsp;
the meeting.&nbsp; So could we then move on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:21.040
Tim and Matt Whitty.&nbsp; And Tim, maybe as a&nbsp;
sort of by way of introduction, you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:21.040 --> 00:29:27.760
just sort of maybe contrast how you see innovation&nbsp;
spreading in the UK across the NHS compared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.640 --> 00:29:34.720
the US where you come from.
&gt;&gt;Timothy Ferris: David, thanks.&nbsp; I'm so pleased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:35.760 --> 00:29:42.400
to have the opportunity to introduce Matt&nbsp;
Whitty to those of you who don't know him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.080 --> 00:29:52.560
and the extraordinary programme that he oversees.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Also important to recognise Lord Darzi's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.400 --> 00:30:02.880
influence on this over decades because I think&nbsp;
it's fair to say that pretty much everything that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:15.040
Matt Whitty is going to describe is a result of&nbsp;
a – let's just say a long-term effort to clarify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:25.440
the process by which innovators, both&nbsp;
externally and internally both test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:26.320 --> 00:30:36.480
pilot-test and get adoption of their innovations.&nbsp;
And so then – so David, to your point about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:45.360
comparing the US, I would say the&nbsp;
classic description is that the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.360 --> 00:30:52.640
adoption of technology is both stochastic,&nbsp;
meaning there's a lot of random motion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.080 --> 00:31:03.840
and it is not particularly rigorous in its&nbsp;
evaluation of the efficacy and effectiveness.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.920 --> 00:31:17.920
Having said that, the classic experience&nbsp;
historically of the NHS is that it has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.920 --> 00:31:29.920
possibly too far the other way, which is overly&nbsp;
rigid and with an unclear process and slow to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.920 --> 00:31:39.200
adopt, in part because the pathways for adoption&nbsp;
were unclear.&nbsp; I think the AAC and Matt Whitty's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:39.200 --> 00:31:46.400
whole team together with Matt have really changed&nbsp;
the face of the NHS with respect to innovation.&nbsp;

00:31:47.040 --> 00:31:53.520
And let me just close by saying, there – I view&nbsp;
two kinds of innovation here.&nbsp; One is innovation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:59.840
that comes in from the outside.&nbsp; So these&nbsp;
are innovators across the biotech landscape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:02.080 --> 00:32:11.440
including devices, diagnostics and all&nbsp;
the things that are at frequent pace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:18.560
entering into the delivery of medical care.&nbsp;&nbsp;
But at the same time, it's very important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.560 --> 00:32:24.320
to remember and acknowledge that an enormous&nbsp;
amount of innovation, generally more focused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:34.800
on process than on devices, comes from inside the&nbsp;
service.&nbsp; And the AHSNs and the other networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:40.880
the innovation networks, the innovation fellowship&nbsp;
programme that the NHS has sponsored have actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.880 --> 00:32:49.040
produced extraordinary innovations from the&nbsp;
people inside of the NHS who are dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:54.880
to that.&nbsp; I know Matt's going to talk more about&nbsp;
that, but I just wanted to emphasise to everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:55.680 --> 00:33:03.680
the dual nature of this, the – both the&nbsp;
outside-in and the inside-in and the scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:03.680 --> 00:33:11.120
of adoption of innovations.&nbsp; Matt, over to you.
&gt;&gt;Matt Whitty: Thank you, Tim, and thank you, Lord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:11.120 --> 00:33:15.600
Prior.&nbsp; And so I've got the incredibly fortunate&nbsp;
position of leading NHS England's Innovation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:20.080
Research and Life Sciences Team, which&nbsp;
hosts the Accelerated Access Collaborative.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:20.080 --> 00:33:22.800
Today I'm going to briefly&nbsp;
cover our purpose, what we do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:28.880
a little bit of the impact that we've had and&nbsp;
our future priorities.&nbsp; So I think importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:28.880 --> 00:33:33.440
starting with our purpose.&nbsp; The Accelerated Access&nbsp;
Collaborative is here to improve patient outcomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:33.440 --> 00:33:38.560
and reduce health inequalities through research&nbsp;
and innovation.&nbsp; And we do this by bringing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:38.560 --> 00:33:45.120
together patient charities, the NHS, government,&nbsp;
regulators and industry to address the challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:45.120 --> 00:33:49.120
in getting more patients involved in&nbsp;
research and the faster adoptive – the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.120 --> 00:33:53.440
faster adoption of innovative medicines, medical&nbsp;
devices, diagnostics and digital products.&nbsp;

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:58.480
And I think the timing of this work is key because&nbsp;
I don't think it's ever been more important.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:59.040 --> 00:34:02.720
Just highlighting one personal example, which&nbsp;
I'm sure is not unique to me and many others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.840 --> 00:34:07.520
on this board will have experienced similar&nbsp;
things, is that my dad was admitted to hospital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:07.520 --> 00:34:12.000
with COVID last month, and he was immediately&nbsp;
asked by the nurse caring for him whether he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:18.000
wanted to be included in a new trial for a COVID&nbsp;
treatment.&nbsp; And this is a clear example of NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:23.440
staff recognising the importance of research and&nbsp;
using that to quickly generate answers about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:23.440 --> 00:34:28.960
works and just as importantly, what doesn't.&nbsp; And&nbsp;
that, the answering those questions, being part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:28.960 --> 00:34:34.080
providing high quality clinical care.&nbsp; And the&nbsp;
NHS staff have undertaken this research through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:34.080 --> 00:34:40.080
the recovery trial and other trials such as REACT&nbsp;
have saved&nbsp;millions of lives across the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:45.680
So these are not hypothetical academic exercises,&nbsp;
these are solving real problems for our patients.&nbsp;

00:34:46.720 --> 00:34:50.560
So I've highlighted quite a lot of examples&nbsp;
of our research and innovation programmes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:50.560 --> 00:34:55.040
the paper.&nbsp; But today I just wanted to briefly&nbsp;
highlight three tangible examples of some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:55.040 --> 00:34:59.760
our work.&nbsp; So first, I wanted to talk a little&nbsp;
bit about the introduction of placental growth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:05.440
factor-based testing.&nbsp; This is a life-saving&nbsp;
world first test for pregnant women that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:05.440 --> 00:35:10.400
rule out pre-eclampsia, which is a potentially&nbsp;
life-threatening condition.&nbsp; And this means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:16.320
up to 65,000 women a year in the NHS, who would&nbsp;
otherwise be admitted to hospital can instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:16.320 --> 00:35:22.880
be quickly tested and safely sent home.&nbsp; And our&nbsp;
AAC-led programmes scaled this from just two NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:22.880 --> 00:35:29.680
trusts in 2019 to over three quarters of maternity&nbsp;
units today.&nbsp; So having these innovations in place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:34.720
through our programmes means that when things like&nbsp;
COVID strike, we are better able to provide the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:34.720 --> 00:35:39.280
care for our patients and safer care that our&nbsp;
clinicians want to provide for our patients.&nbsp;

00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:45.280
Second example is alongside colleagues&nbsp;
that we work with really closely from NHSX,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:45.280 --> 00:35:50.160
through our Artificial Intelligence in Health and&nbsp;
Care Award programme, we've supported clinicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:50.160 --> 00:35:54.960
in Reading and Oxford to develop and test&nbsp;
Brainomix.&nbsp; This is an artificial intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:54.960 --> 00:36:00.000
tool that speeds up the reading of brain scans&nbsp;
and recent results from these sites have shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:04.720
a reduction of more than an hour for patients&nbsp;
in the diagnosis and treatment of stroke since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:04.720 --> 00:36:09.680
they introduced the technology.&nbsp; And they've also&nbsp;
seen a three-fold increase in patients being able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:09.680 --> 00:36:14.720
to achieve independence following a stroke.&nbsp; So&nbsp;
these are innovations that are being developed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:14.720 --> 00:36:20.880
tested in the NHS, but they're already starting to&nbsp;
show meaningful clinical impact for our patients.&nbsp;

00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:27.200
The third example I wanted to highlight was&nbsp;
following on from the NHS long-term plan, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:27.200 --> 00:36:32.640
identified population health and preventative&nbsp;
medicine as a key area.&nbsp; And one of those areas of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:32.640 --> 00:36:37.680
focus was around the earlier diagnosis of cancer.&nbsp;&nbsp;
And we've worked with NHS England's cancer team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:37.680 --> 00:36:43.200
and the Commercial Medicines Directorate on a&nbsp;
deal for a new cancer blood test, which is GRAIL's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:49.200
Galleri blood test.&nbsp; So this test can detect&nbsp;
over 50 different types of cancer.&nbsp; Now, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:49.200 --> 00:36:58.640
part – part of the deal was this involved a huge&nbsp;
160,000 patient trial in the NHS, and importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:58.640 --> 00:37:02.720
this is prioritised in the areas of the country&nbsp;
with the highest needs.&nbsp; So there are areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:02.720 --> 00:37:08.800
of the country where you are more likely to be&nbsp;
[inaudible] with cancer at a late stage.&nbsp; So this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:08.800 --> 00:37:14.960
trial has prioritised patients from those areas.&nbsp;&nbsp;
So this really highlights in practice our strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.960 --> 00:37:20.800
commitment to reducing health inequalities.&nbsp;
Alongside the size of this trial and the targeted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.800 --> 00:37:26.160
nature for patients with – from more deprived&nbsp;
backgrounds and who have got poorer outcomes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:26.160 --> 00:37:30.960
one of the important things I want to highlight is&nbsp;
the set time for this trial was extremely fast.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:31.680 --> 00:37:36.880
The UK average time for setting up a trial&nbsp;
from submission of the regulatory paperwork&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:36.880 --> 00:37:42.560
to the first patient being enrolled in the&nbsp;
trial is around 218 days.&nbsp; For this NHS trial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:42.560 --> 00:37:47.680
we did it in 35 days.&nbsp; And we did that by&nbsp;
taking lessons from the vaccine and the COVID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:47.680 --> 00:37:52.320
treatment studies, some of which I mentioned&nbsp;
earlier.&nbsp; We've also worked with colleagues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:52.320 --> 00:37:55.680
clinical colleagues on the Commercial&nbsp;
Medicines Directorate on other significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:55.680 --> 00:38:01.200
population health large deals, such as for the&nbsp;
cholesterol drug inclisiran, which is part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:06.000
our broader work supporting all NICE-approved&nbsp;
treatments for cholesterol management as well.&nbsp;

00:38:07.520 --> 00:38:12.480
Just to give a bit of an idea about the impact of&nbsp;
our work.&nbsp; These are not niche projects.&nbsp; These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:12.480 --> 00:38:18.160
are huge projects.&nbsp; So over 1.4&nbsp;million patients&nbsp;
have benefited from our programmes to date.&nbsp; And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:24.080
over the last year, we've helped patients spend&nbsp;
over 140,000 fewer days in hospital.&nbsp; We've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:31.920
supported over 3,700 innovations across 700 sites&nbsp;
across health and care.&nbsp; We've also helped support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:31.920 --> 00:38:38.640
around 3,500 innovators, including over 700&nbsp;
staff as part of our NHS Clinical Entrepreneurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.640 --> 00:38:44.800
Programme, which provides mentoring and advice to&nbsp;
NHS staff to enable them to develop solutions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:44.800 --> 00:38:49.120
the problems that they can see on the frontline&nbsp;
and help them to do that whilst remaining in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:49.120 --> 00:38:53.440
NHS, so that they don't feel they have to leave&nbsp;
and join a tech company or join their own start-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.320 --> 00:38:57.280
in order to develop solutions that they&nbsp;
want to provide for their patients.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:57.840 --> 00:39:02.240
And we've also saved NHS organisations&nbsp;
over £119&nbsp;million in the last year.&nbsp;

00:39:03.440 --> 00:39:07.520
So work I've outlined is clearly just the start&nbsp;
and the Accelerated Access Collaborative has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:07.520 --> 00:39:11.440
identified six priorities to build on this&nbsp;
so that we can do more for our patients.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:12.240 --> 00:39:17.200
So firstly, we are committed to providing a&nbsp;
clearer articulation of NHS needs, something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:17.200 --> 00:39:22.160
that we call demand signalling, and matching&nbsp;
these needs to a systematic search for solutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:22.160 --> 00:39:27.520
which we call horizon scanning.&nbsp; And we do this&nbsp;
by working with our partners across the AAC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.520 --> 00:39:32.240
in the Medicines and Healthcare Regulatory&nbsp;
Authority, NICE and many other partners as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:32.240 --> 00:39:36.160
And this means that we can better prepare for&nbsp;
the next generation of innovations coming through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:40.240
and stimulate innovation and research in&nbsp;
the highest priority areas for our patients.&nbsp;

00:39:41.280 --> 00:39:46.560
Second, we want to increase the diversity, scale&nbsp;
and speed of research.&nbsp; I've given one example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.560 --> 00:39:50.080
with the GRAIL test about how we're doing that&nbsp;
in practice, but we want to do more so that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:50.080 --> 00:39:55.200
NHS can have the clinical evidence that better&nbsp;
reflects our population and our health system.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:55.200 --> 00:39:58.720
And that will make it easier to adopt the&nbsp;
best innovations that come out of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:59.760 --> 00:40:04.400
research, and that will ultimately help to improve&nbsp;
outcomes and to reduce health inequalities.&nbsp;

00:40:05.600 --> 00:40:09.600
Third, we're ensuring that we've got programmes&nbsp;
to support the uptake of NICE-approved proven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:09.600 --> 00:40:16.160
innovation on the NHS – in the NHS.&nbsp; And this is&nbsp;
supporting work for mapping out the pathway that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:16.720 --> 00:40:22.000
– from ideation to adoption across&nbsp;
medicines, diagnostics, medical devices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:26.560
and digital products, alongside a range of&nbsp;
support programmes for NHS staff to help them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:31.120
adopt these proven innovations.
Fourth, we want to support the NHS workforce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:31.120 --> 00:40:34.960
to champion innovation on the frontline.&nbsp; I've&nbsp;
mentioned our workforce support programme, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:34.960 --> 00:40:39.360
clinical entrepreneurs programme, but we also want&nbsp;
to make it easier for staff to undertake research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:39.360 --> 00:40:42.080
that I mentioned earlier as well,&nbsp;
and to help them implement these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.080 --> 00:40:44.800
new innovations and provide the care&nbsp;
that they want to for their patients.&nbsp;

00:40:45.840 --> 00:40:49.520
Fifthly, we're committed to continuing our&nbsp;
collaborative approach to transforming care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:49.520 --> 00:40:53.760
across all of our AAC partners.&nbsp; So some of&nbsp;
this will be through the at-scale commercial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:53.760 --> 00:40:58.160
deals that I mentioned.&nbsp; But it'll also be&nbsp;
through things like our NHS innovation service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:58.160 --> 00:41:02.640
which is a portal that we're launching to make&nbsp;
it easier for innovators to navigate what can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:06.960
quite a complicated landscape in the NHS, so that&nbsp;
they can get the right support at the right time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:06.960 --> 00:41:11.600
to develop the future solutions that we know we&nbsp;
will need.&nbsp; And we work closely, as Tim mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.600 --> 00:41:17.120
with the Academic Health Science Networks as part&nbsp;
of spreading best practice and rapidly testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:17.120 --> 00:41:21.200
the new innovations that have coming through.
And six, and probably most importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:21.200 --> 00:41:26.240
meaningfully involving patients and the public in&nbsp;
our work.&nbsp; We've recently agreed our patient and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:26.240 --> 00:41:30.960
public involvement strategy to ensure we've got&nbsp;
a diverse range of patients to help us do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:35.360
so that we can proactively address equality&nbsp;
and diversity and inclusion in our work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:35.360 --> 00:41:41.040
and that we can actually support patients.&nbsp; So&nbsp;
these are not the case of just token patient on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:45.520
a panel, for example.&nbsp; They are actively seen&nbsp;
as part of our team supported, and enabled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:49.760
to help make meaningful contributions.&nbsp; We're&nbsp;
also working on how we can measure the impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.760 --> 00:41:54.480
of this, and we're also working with patients to&nbsp;
help better communicate the impact of our work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:55.200 --> 00:41:58.160
So those are the areas that I wanted to&nbsp;
cover.&nbsp; I'm happy to take any questions.&nbsp;

00:41:58.160 --> 00:42:03.600
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Matt, thank you very&nbsp;
much for that.&nbsp; I'd just like to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.600 --> 00:42:07.840
kick it off by just saying, I think you've done&nbsp;
a fabulous job, you and your team at the AAC.&nbsp; I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:07.840 --> 00:42:14.080
think for many years, the NHS has been caricatured&nbsp;
really as a very slow, difficult adopter.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:14.960 --> 00:42:21.040
You've started to really knock a lot of those&nbsp;
barriers down, and it's terrific.&nbsp; So just one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.040 --> 00:42:26.000
question, you gave the example of your father&nbsp;
coming into the NHS.&nbsp; I mean, we ought to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:26.000 --> 00:42:32.320
giving the opportunity to everybody coming into&nbsp;
NHS hospitals to join a clinical trial because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:32.320 --> 00:42:37.520
I mean, that is how we learn.&nbsp; It's how we get&nbsp;
– how healthcare improves.&nbsp; And I think we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:37.520 --> 00:42:42.800
got – still got a lot of work to do to persuade,&nbsp;
I know very, very busy hospitals and very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:42.800 --> 00:42:48.800
very busy clinicians, that actually a real&nbsp;
commitment to research can pay dividends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:50.720 --> 00:42:54.480
Ruth and then Wol.&nbsp; Ruth.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you, Chair.&nbsp; So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.480 --> 00:43:01.280
I very much welcome this.&nbsp; It's wonderful to&nbsp;
see this innovation and seeing it going into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:01.280 --> 00:43:07.680
practice and that's what's making a difference&nbsp;
to our patients and also rewarding the staff.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:12.080
So as Tim knows, and thank you, Matt,&nbsp;
for referencing it in the paper, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:12.880 --> 00:43:19.440
on this Monday, the 22nd of November, I launched&nbsp;
the very first chief nursing officer research plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:19.440 --> 00:43:25.360
and had five areas within that.&nbsp; The first was&nbsp;
aligning nurse-led research with the public need,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:25.920 --> 00:43:31.760
much of what you've talked about already.&nbsp; Second&nbsp;
was releasing nurses' research potential so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:31.760 --> 00:43:38.160
they become part of the researching rather than&nbsp;
supporting research, as well as that.&nbsp; Third was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:43.920
building the best research system, then developing&nbsp;
future nurse leaders of research.&nbsp; And finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:43.920 --> 00:43:49.680
just as important, is digitally-enabled nurse-led&nbsp;
research.&nbsp; So it's gone down very, very well with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:49.680 --> 00:43:55.360
the profession.&nbsp; And I see that as something that&nbsp;
is going to be key to supporting this work, Matt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:55.360 --> 00:43:59.200
and Tim.&nbsp; Thank you, Chair.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:59.200 --> 00:44:02.160
Thank you, Ruth.&nbsp; Wol.
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Thanks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:02.160 --> 00:44:08.640
David.&nbsp; I completely agree with David's earlier&nbsp;
comments about the amazing work your team has done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:08.640 --> 00:44:14.560
in such a short space of time, and the evidence is&nbsp;
there in the outputs, which have been fantastic.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:15.440 --> 00:44:22.400
This may be a sort of down the road&nbsp;
type issue, but I'm wondering, given the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:23.840 --> 00:44:29.520
vast amounts of money being put into healthcare&nbsp;
generally by private equity and venture capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:30.640 --> 00:44:38.320
and the incredible interest in biotech, how we can&nbsp;
get to sort of risk-sharing perspective with the –&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:38.320 --> 00:44:42.640
with those people.&nbsp; So we've got a finite amount&nbsp;
of money.&nbsp; We don't want to see any pressure.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:42.640 --> 00:44:47.200
And yet sometimes you need to move faster than we&nbsp;
can move with the small pot of money you've got.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:48.080 --> 00:44:50.400
What thoughts do you have about sort&nbsp;
of risk share at the front end?&nbsp; I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:50.400 --> 00:44:53.600
know about the commercialisation, but&nbsp;
actually at the very beginning.&nbsp; Thanks.&nbsp;

00:44:53.600 --> 00:44:59.040
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Matt.
&gt;&gt;Matt Whitty:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:01.680
Thanks, Wol.&nbsp; It's a really, really&nbsp;
interesting question, and I know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:02.400 --> 00:45:09.440
as part of the life sciences vision, access&nbsp;
to capital was also mentioned as a key area.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:09.440 --> 00:45:14.400
So one of the areas where we actually started&nbsp;
thinking about doing that is actually how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:14.400 --> 00:45:20.080
work with investors so that they can actually see&nbsp;
we want them to be making similar investments that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:20.080 --> 00:45:23.120
are important to us.&nbsp; So I think our demand&nbsp;
signalling work that I mentioned earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:23.120 --> 00:45:26.800
sort of getting better at articulating what&nbsp;
our needs are, will help investors make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:26.800 --> 00:45:30.560
better decisions, so they're placing bets&nbsp;
in areas that we're also interested in.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:31.120 --> 00:45:36.160
We haven't explored sort of early stage sort of&nbsp;
joint ventures and so forth to do that as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:36.800 --> 00:45:40.080
We have started talking to investors to&nbsp;
understand how they make decisions.&nbsp; What's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:40.080 --> 00:45:44.320
important to them, what information sources&nbsp;
that they can see, and help then feed that into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:44.320 --> 00:45:47.440
the other part of that work I mentioned, our&nbsp;
horizon scanning, so we can start to see how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:47.440 --> 00:45:51.520
do investors think, so that we can start to do&nbsp;
that.&nbsp; But we don't have any specific plans to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:51.520 --> 00:45:55.840
do joint investments at the moment around that as&nbsp;
well.&nbsp; But it's a particularly interesting area.&nbsp;

00:45:55.840 --> 00:46:00.000
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Sorry, David, just a tiny&nbsp;
follow-up.&nbsp; Matt, if you – given my background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:03.360
which is private equity, if you&nbsp;
need any help thinking that through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:03.360 --> 00:46:06.080
if you want someone to come and just sort of&nbsp;
sense-shuffle with you, I'm delighted to help.&nbsp;

00:46:06.640 --> 00:46:09.200
&gt;&gt;Matt Whitty: Fantastic.&nbsp; Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:09.200 --> 00:46:14.720
thanks very much, Wol.&nbsp; Munir.
&gt;&gt;Munir Pirmohamed: Thank you very much Matt.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:14.720 --> 00:46:21.760
A great paper and good to see that research and&nbsp;
innovation being discussed at the NHS board.&nbsp; I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:21.760 --> 00:46:27.840
got two questions.&nbsp; The first relates to uptake&nbsp;
of NICE-approved technologies within the NHS.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:30.080 --> 00:46:38.000
It's getting better, but it's still not – it's&nbsp;
still below 60%, I think, within the framework.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:38.880 --> 00:46:42.560
Can you – have you – is your team doing&nbsp;
any work to find out what is it that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:42.560 --> 00:46:46.160
prevents people from taking up&nbsp;
technologies?&nbsp; There may be different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:46.160 --> 00:46:49.280
things for different technologies, but&nbsp;
are there some general lessons that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:49.280 --> 00:46:53.920
can learn from why it's so difficult to&nbsp;
take up new technologies into the NHS?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:54.800 --> 00:46:59.920
The second question relates to research in&nbsp;
the NHS and being embedded within the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:59.920 --> 00:47:03.600
which is really, really important.&nbsp; We've&nbsp;
got an opportunity with the new integrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:03.600 --> 00:47:10.800
care systems coming online, and what can we&nbsp;
do to make sure that ICSs have research as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:10.800 --> 00:47:17.600
business as usual as part of this setup?
&gt;&gt;Matt Whitty: Thank you.&nbsp; So I'll take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:17.600 --> 00:47:22.960
the second question first if that's okay, in&nbsp;
terms of working with integrated care systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:22.960 --> 00:47:27.760
So we've been working really closely with the&nbsp;
teams developing integrated care systems.&nbsp; And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:27.760 --> 00:47:31.760
particularly, looking at job descriptions for&nbsp;
chairs and chief executives to make sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:31.760 --> 00:47:36.000
research and innovation are included as part&nbsp;
of those.&nbsp; We also work really closely with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:36.000 --> 00:47:40.720
the regional medical directors and their teams&nbsp;
so that when we're actually looking to roll out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:40.720 --> 00:47:44.080
innovations, that they're actually aware of kind&nbsp;
of what the innovations are that we're working on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:44.080 --> 00:47:48.240
and that they're aware of our programmes as&nbsp;
well.&nbsp; We know that there's plenty more to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:48.240 --> 00:47:53.200
with integrated care systems, particularly as&nbsp;
sort of a whole new range of leaders have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:53.200 --> 00:47:57.520
announced over the last couple of weeks and as&nbsp;
they begin, there'll be plenty more work for us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:57.520 --> 00:48:01.280
to do with integrated care systems.
And in terms of the adoption of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:02.800 --> 00:48:06.480
NICE-approved treatments, it is a challenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;
I mean, international comparisons are always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:06.480 --> 00:48:10.960
difficult with different patient populations and&nbsp;
so forth.&nbsp; So it's sometimes quite difficult to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:10.960 --> 00:48:17.040
get sort of a like-for-like comparison.&nbsp; So it's&nbsp;
important to have that important caveat.&nbsp; There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:17.040 --> 00:48:22.160
are a number of things that we need to look at.&nbsp;&nbsp;
So particularly for medical devices, often the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:22.160 --> 00:48:27.520
implementation is more complicated because there's&nbsp;
often a bigger associated service change that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:27.520 --> 00:48:32.400
required.&nbsp; So the implementation times often take&nbsp;
much longer if you're sort of switching from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:33.280 --> 00:48:40.480
model of sort of inpatient care to day-case&nbsp;
type care, that can take significantly longer.&nbsp;

00:48:40.480 --> 00:48:45.920
We've got a range of programmes providing&nbsp;
bespoke support for technologies that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:45.920 --> 00:48:49.760
NICE-approved that have lower – have the lowest&nbsp;
uptake, where we think we can make an impact, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:49.760 --> 00:48:53.840
that's something called our rapid uptake product.&nbsp;&nbsp;
So we have tailored support where we work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:54.400 --> 00:48:58.400
clinicians, we work with patient groups to&nbsp;
understand what the barriers are on a case by case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:58.400 --> 00:49:03.280
basis.&nbsp; And we feed those lessons into&nbsp;
our general learning at the AAC board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:03.280 --> 00:49:09.520
and obviously, NICE are one of the members of the&nbsp;
board.&nbsp; And then that further feeds the materials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:09.520 --> 00:49:15.920
that NICE produce to support adoption as well.&nbsp;
We also look at things where they haven't worked.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:15.920 --> 00:49:20.320
They haven't – one of the examples where we&nbsp;
looked at that is around some of the lipid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:20.320 --> 00:49:25.760
modifying treatments.&nbsp; So you see with some of&nbsp;
the agents that people go on to after statins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:25.760 --> 00:49:29.440
and ezetimibe, for example, haven't worked.&nbsp;&nbsp;
And we've looked at where – the reasons why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:29.440 --> 00:49:34.400
they've had lower uptake.&nbsp; And that has also fed&nbsp;
into why we've done commercial deals for newer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:34.400 --> 00:49:37.920
things coming through to try and instead of wait&nbsp;
for things to actually get to the stage of not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:37.920 --> 00:49:42.080
having the level of adoption we want, to try and&nbsp;
address these proactively in real-time before they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:42.720 --> 00:49:47.280
happen as well.&nbsp; But it's an extremely challenging&nbsp;
piece of work.&nbsp; And we've got plenty more to do to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:47.280 --> 00:49:52.000
understand those as well.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:52.000 --> 00:49:57.360
Julia then David Behan.
&gt;&gt;Julia Goodfellow: Thank you, Matt.&nbsp; Very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:57.360 --> 00:50:02.800
interesting and fantastic work.&nbsp; I'm sure you have&nbsp;
a lot of people wanting your services, and I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:02.800 --> 00:50:09.360
wondered how you prioritise?&nbsp; What do you do?&nbsp; Are&nbsp;
you getting too much call on your time and your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:09.360 --> 00:50:15.120
resources?&nbsp; I just wondered how you approach that?
&gt;&gt;Matt Whitty: Well, I'd say that I'm not unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:15.120 --> 00:50:18.560
in the NHS in having lots of calls on my time,&nbsp;
and everyone on this call and many people watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:18.560 --> 00:50:22.640
will have many, many – far too many calls on their&nbsp;
time as well.&nbsp; But I think that the – probably the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:22.640 --> 00:50:28.320
bigger worry is where you don't have those calls&nbsp;
on your time as well.&nbsp; In terms of the sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:28.320 --> 00:50:32.800
the overall priorities for the AAC, we obviously&nbsp;
work across government and across our different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:32.800 --> 00:50:36.960
partners to kind of agree the priority areas&nbsp;
where we think we can have the biggest impacts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:36.960 --> 00:50:41.840
And if you mean around sort of specific deals&nbsp;
and so forth, some of those bigger deals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:42.400 --> 00:50:47.040
we work closely with colleagues in finance&nbsp;
and with our clinical colleagues across NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:47.040 --> 00:50:50.960
England to look at where are these deals – where&nbsp;
can we actually make a big impact?&nbsp; You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:50.960 --> 00:50:54.480
these deals take a huge amount of resource&nbsp;
internally to do.&nbsp; So we need to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:54.480 --> 00:50:57.440
they're focused on areas where there is&nbsp;
actually a genuine need.&nbsp; So what is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:57.440 --> 00:51:01.040
link to the long-term power plan and our strategic&nbsp;
games?&nbsp; Where can we actually make a difference?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:01.040 --> 00:51:04.160
Do we need to do a deal or will something just go&nbsp;
through NICE anyway, and we actually don't need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:04.160 --> 00:51:08.000
intervene?&nbsp; Or actually, would it – us intervening&nbsp;
actually slow things down and not make any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:08.000 --> 00:51:12.320
impact?&nbsp; And where we sort of start to tick those&nbsp;
boxes, then we can actually start to look at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:12.320 --> 00:51:15.520
where do we want to do a deal?&nbsp; Is there a&nbsp;
potential for us to benefit from that as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:15.520 --> 00:51:16.800
But it tends to – it's got to have a clinical&nbsp;
sponsor leadership.&nbsp; It's got to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:20.640 --> 00:51:23.920
clear evidence based that actually this is the&nbsp;
right thing to do.&nbsp; It's got to be in strategic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:23.920 --> 00:51:30.000
alignment with our clinical aims.&nbsp; And there's&nbsp;
also got to be a partner who's willing to enter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:33.920
a really tough negotiate – tough negotiation&nbsp;
with us and actually to put in the work to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:33.920 --> 00:51:36.880
actually come up with an innovative model that&nbsp;
is going to make a difference to patients.&nbsp;

00:51:39.600 --> 00:51:42.800
&gt;&gt;David Prior: David.
&gt;&gt;David Behan: Thanks, David.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:43.600 --> 00:51:46.000
Matt, thank you for this and Tim, thank you.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:47.840 --> 00:51:54.000
Tim, I thought your framing of outside-in and&nbsp;
inside-in was really helpful.&nbsp; And the examples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:54.000 --> 00:52:01.440
Matt, in the paper, I found very powerful and very&nbsp;
compelling.&nbsp; But I wonder whether you could say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:01.440 --> 00:52:07.200
a bit more about the process improvements and&nbsp;
innovation?&nbsp; I'm trying to connect the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:07.200 --> 00:52:12.640
report that we had Mark Cubbon took us through in&nbsp;
terms of what the reality is at the present time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:13.280 --> 00:52:21.600
and how innovation might not just be additive&nbsp;
in terms of adding new tests and techniques and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:22.320 --> 00:52:27.760
equipment, but how we might improve both the&nbsp;
productivity and the efficiency of what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:28.640 --> 00:52:34.000
are offering and therefore, some of the material&nbsp;
that we had at the beginning of the meeting about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:34.000 --> 00:52:40.640
what the current reality is in terms of waiting&nbsp;
times, et cetera.&nbsp; How innovation research might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:40.640 --> 00:52:46.880
be contributing to that as well.&nbsp; And it just&nbsp;
strikes me that's an important dimension of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:46.880 --> 00:52:53.040
innovation and research that you – that we might&nbsp;
give some oxygen and some sunlight and water to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:53.040 --> 00:52:58.800
grow that a little bit more.&nbsp; Thank you, David.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:58.800 --> 00:53:05.680
Matt or Tim.&nbsp; Tim, you're raising your hand.
&gt;&gt;Timothy Ferris: Yeah.&nbsp; I'm so glad you asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:05.680 --> 00:53:12.400
that, David.&nbsp; It's a great question.&nbsp; And one&nbsp;
of the – earlier on, in Amanda's comments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:12.400 --> 00:53:20.160
she talked about organisational changes that&nbsp;
we are working on and planning.&nbsp; Matt and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:20.800 --> 00:53:32.000
see extensive opportunities for the processes,&nbsp;
platforms that he has been working on to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:32.000 --> 00:53:37.760
greater integration with operations.&nbsp; And I want&nbsp;
to just connect that directly to what Mark Cubbon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:38.400 --> 00:53:49.440
presented.&nbsp; And David, I'm a big believer, I know&nbsp;
you are, in the importance of promoting agency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:49.440 --> 00:53:56.400
within our workforce.&nbsp; And here, what I'm talking&nbsp;
about is, for example, one small example is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:56.400 --> 00:54:05.520
we are about to launch a website for – to collect&nbsp;
ideas from the people working throughout the NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:06.160 --> 00:54:15.440
around our top priorities.&nbsp; And we will have a&nbsp;
curation process and a pilot and support process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:15.440 --> 00:54:21.040
associated with that.&nbsp; And I think that is&nbsp;
one of the direct advantages of some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:21.040 --> 00:54:28.400
organisational changes that we are contemplating.&nbsp;
So I just want to – so connecting those two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:28.400 --> 00:54:35.600
conversations, and I can't let that question pass,&nbsp;
David, without also mentioning the importance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:35.600 --> 00:54:44.080
when I said inside-in, what I meant by inside&nbsp;
included our close partners in social care.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:44.880 --> 00:54:49.040
Some of our biggest challenges&nbsp;
associated with hospital capacity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:50.240 --> 00:54:56.800
and frail elderly, the numerous challenges&nbsp;
that have already been discussed by Pauline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:58.080 --> 00:55:04.400
we will need the service, the people delivering&nbsp;
the care to be part of a process for innovation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:04.400 --> 00:55:12.720
because they are the best capable of understanding&nbsp;
those problems and suggesting alternatives.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:12.720 --> 00:55:22.800
And we need to support a process for those ideas&nbsp;
to come forth and to test.&nbsp; That's – I recognise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:22.800 --> 00:55:29.200
that is not a small undertaking, what I've just&nbsp;
described, but I think it's a very important one.&nbsp;

00:55:29.200 --> 00:55:38.880
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Tim.&nbsp; Julian.
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: Matt, I recently visited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:38.880 --> 00:55:43.360
Cambridge and the team under Rebecca Fitzgerald&nbsp;
that sort of developed the cytosponge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:45.280 --> 00:55:50.800
technique.&nbsp; And she was explaining to me&nbsp;
like the 20-year journey that she has been on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:51.920 --> 00:55:56.960
to get that thing designed and&nbsp;
tested.&nbsp; And I sort of wonder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:58.560 --> 00:56:03.760
what lessons do you think we can learn about&nbsp;
sort of innovation in areas which isn't at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:03.760 --> 00:56:08.720
the complete high-end, but it's the kind of vast&nbsp;
simplification of what are otherwise incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:08.720 --> 00:56:15.040
complicated and costly techniques?&nbsp; And do&nbsp;
you think there's more we can do to encourage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:15.040 --> 00:56:18.640
thinking in that area?
&gt;&gt;Matt Whitty:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:18.640 --> 00:56:24.000
Yeah, I think that's a great example, and it's&nbsp;
sadly often a fairly typical sort of journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:24.960 --> 00:56:30.640
timeline.&nbsp; There's two answers I'd give to that,&nbsp;
Julian.&nbsp; So firstly we are currently mapping out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:30.640 --> 00:56:34.080
exactly – this was the point I made earlier – some&nbsp;
of the ideations to actually adoption for medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:34.080 --> 00:56:38.240
technologies.&nbsp; So we can actually understand what&nbsp;
is every single stage these have to go through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:38.240 --> 00:56:43.120
from sort of ideation, testing, regulatory,&nbsp;
CE-marking right through to the regulatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:43.120 --> 00:56:47.360
processes and where is it actually we can most&nbsp;
usefully intervene at the moment?&nbsp; Because we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:47.360 --> 00:56:50.960
had a range of programmes that we've worked on&nbsp;
over the years, which have been giving us all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:50.960 --> 00:56:54.000
the great numbers that I've spoken about, some&nbsp;
great patient impact, we were talking about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:54.000 --> 00:56:58.160
but we haven't really started from where can we&nbsp;
actually have the biggest impact and what's that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:58.160 --> 00:57:01.760
sort of strategic approach.&nbsp; That's – that piece&nbsp;
of work is live at the moment, we're going through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:01.760 --> 00:57:06.960
and we're working with AAC partners to do that.
The other thing I'd say is that in AAC when we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:06.960 --> 00:57:12.400
working with medical technologies companies in&nbsp;
particular, is that there can – you can enter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:12.400 --> 00:57:16.640
sort of a vicious cycle of actually going down&nbsp;
the wrong route or getting the wrong advice and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:16.640 --> 00:57:19.920
so forth.&nbsp; And I think that's why the innovation&nbsp;
service is so important, so that the people can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:19.920 --> 00:57:23.120
actually access to understand what is the&nbsp;
level of research that we need, or what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:23.120 --> 00:57:27.040
the evidence generated I'm going to need to help&nbsp;
persuade innovators.&nbsp; Or how do I get into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:27.040 --> 00:57:32.400
NICE process or how can I approach the National&nbsp;
Institute for Health Research for research on our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:32.400 --> 00:57:36.960
advice as to how to research?&nbsp; So effectively&nbsp;
trying to cut out time that these companies cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:36.960 --> 00:57:41.920
afford to have, otherwise they will not survive.
And thirdly, I think is the – sort of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:41.920 --> 00:57:47.040
commercial angle that actually they have to remain&nbsp;
focused on what the problem is they're trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:47.600 --> 00:57:51.520
solve.&nbsp; And it can sometimes be a little bit of&nbsp;
a disconnect for companies, particularly when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:51.520 --> 00:57:54.560
there are sort of central grants or&nbsp;
central funding and so forth that actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:55.600 --> 00:58:00.320
the customer should always be what is the sort&nbsp;
of the local NHS customer's problem that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:00.320 --> 00:58:04.480
trying to solve.&nbsp; And sometimes the sort of&nbsp;
central national funding can be fantastic for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:04.480 --> 00:58:09.200
company surviving and needing, but you cannot take&nbsp;
your eye off the ball on the main central problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:09.200 --> 00:58:13.200
that you're trying to do and become a sort of&nbsp;
actively a grant winning machine instead of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:13.200 --> 00:58:16.960
patient problem solving machine as well.&nbsp; And&nbsp;
that's a really important point to make as well.&nbsp;

00:58:20.400 --> 00:58:24.720
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Matt, I think that's probably&nbsp;
it.&nbsp; Thank you very much for that for that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:24.720 --> 00:58:28.560
for providing us with that paper,&nbsp;
and on behalf of the board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:28.560 --> 00:58:32.400
you know – oh, Wol, you've put your hand up.
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: I do apologise.&nbsp; I do apologise.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:32.400 --> 00:58:37.200
And this may be again a tiny point, but I just&nbsp;
wondered if you could just explain the difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:38.000 --> 00:58:45.120
or how you interface with – I think it's called&nbsp;
the Transformation Unit, the horizons bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:45.120 --> 00:58:49.520
because they talk about large-scale innovation&nbsp;
and sort of making that happen as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:49.520 --> 00:58:53.040
What's the crossover – it's Helen Bevan, I&nbsp;
think is the one who – she runs that one.&nbsp;

00:58:53.040 --> 00:58:59.120
&gt;&gt;Matt Whitty: Yes.&nbsp; So Helen – after you, Tim.
&gt;&gt;Timothy Ferris: Well, Wol, this is just my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:59.120 --> 00:59:08.160
point.&nbsp; So they have been separate, relatively&nbsp;
separate organisational approaches to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:08.160 --> 00:59:14.000
innovation.&nbsp; They are coming together in the&nbsp;
Transformation Directorate now.&nbsp; They will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:14.000 --> 00:59:20.960
very shortly be fully integrated.&nbsp; How's that?
&gt;&gt;Matt Whitty: One practical example, Wol, of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:20.960 --> 00:59:25.840
we work together is – links into some of the&nbsp;
work that team and Hugh's[?] team led around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:25.840 --> 00:59:31.120
beneficial changes.&nbsp; So they led a huge programme&nbsp;
of work engaging with the frontline last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:31.120 --> 00:59:37.600
identified around 3000 innovations.&nbsp; And what my&nbsp;
team did was support them to work with regional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:37.600 --> 00:59:42.480
medical directors to actually prioritise this long&nbsp;
list of several thousand potentially beneficial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:43.040 --> 00:59:46.960
innovations.&nbsp; And my team also then interfaced&nbsp;
with the National Institute for Health Research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:46.960 --> 00:59:50.880
who control an infrastructure called the&nbsp;
Applied Research Collaborative.&nbsp; So these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:50.880 --> 00:59:55.040
are organisations with huge experience in&nbsp;
evaluation, and they started to evaluate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:55.040 --> 00:59:59.280
this prioritised list, and then my team also&nbsp;
commissioned the Academic Health Science Networks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:59.840 --> 01:00:02.560
to work with actually implementing the&nbsp;
technologies that came out of that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:02.560 --> 01:00:05.040
were good as well.&nbsp; So it was actually – so we&nbsp;
worked together really nicely and that's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:05.040 --> 01:00:07.920
one practical example of how we do that.
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Brilliant, thank you.&nbsp;

01:00:07.920 --> 01:00:11.200
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Matt.&nbsp; I think it&nbsp;
just worth saying that Matt is organising&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:11.200 --> 01:00:18.080
a series of visits to all the Academic Health&nbsp;
Science Networks, the AHSNs.&nbsp; If any directors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:18.080 --> 01:00:22.000
non-execs or exec directors would like&nbsp;
to join those meetings, I'm sure, Matt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:22.000 --> 01:00:25.040
they'd be very welcome,&nbsp; wouldn't they?
&gt;&gt;Matt Whitty: It will be great to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:25.040 --> 01:00:27.200
as many of you as we can.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Brilliant.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:27.200 --> 01:00:33.120
Good.&nbsp; Thank you very much, Matt.&nbsp; Great&nbsp;
paper.&nbsp; Great presentation.&nbsp; Thank you.&nbsp; So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:35.280 --> 01:00:41.840
moving seamlessly on to mental health, Claire,&nbsp;
I can't see you, but I think you're with us.&nbsp;

01:00:41.840 --> 01:00:45.040
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Good afternoon.&nbsp; I'm&nbsp;
with you and I'm with my colleague,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:45.040 --> 01:00:49.280
Professor Tim Kendall.&nbsp; We're both here.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Hi, Tim as well.&nbsp; Good to have you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:49.280 --> 01:00:53.840
both on the call.&nbsp; So Claire, are you kicking off?
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: I am, and then I will hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:53.840 --> 01:00:59.120
briefly to Tim talk about digital and&nbsp;
innovation as well.&nbsp; So obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:59.120 --> 01:01:05.600
people have read the paper, but just to remind&nbsp;
colleagues that pre-COVID, we had a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:05.600 --> 01:01:12.160
ambitious long-term plan for mental health,&nbsp;
where we committed to seeing 2&nbsp;million more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:12.160 --> 01:01:19.920
patients a year every year by 2023-24.&nbsp; So our&nbsp;
long-term plan and we had clear investment on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:19.920 --> 01:01:25.520
and workforce trajectories and everything else&nbsp;
that you'd expect to underpin a plan such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:25.520 --> 01:01:31.040
that that we intended to deliver.&nbsp; I think it's&nbsp;
fair to say that we were well on track with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:31.040 --> 01:01:36.480
implementation of our plan.&nbsp; Just to really&nbsp;
remind colleagues, of course, that one would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:36.480 --> 01:01:41.920
never talk – just talk about physical health plan&nbsp;
in the way we do a mental health plan, to reassure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:41.920 --> 01:01:48.640
everybody under that sit different patient groups,&nbsp;
different pathways, and there are examples of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:48.640 --> 01:01:54.880
those in the paper.&nbsp; So it's an all-age strategy.
We were making good progress.&nbsp; COVID came and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:54.880 --> 01:01:59.520
as with the rest of society and rest&nbsp;
of the NHS, we were impacted by it.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:00.960 --> 01:02:06.720
In some ways we were impacted in a good way in&nbsp;
terms of the programme because we pulled forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:07.280 --> 01:02:18.080
certain deliverables from 2023-24 to last year and&nbsp;
this.&nbsp; For example, the 24/7 all age crisis lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:18.080 --> 01:02:25.680
that we said the NHS would offer in all parts of&nbsp;
the country.&nbsp; Those crisis lines were mobilised in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:25.680 --> 01:02:30.480
a matter of weeks where they didn't exist before.&nbsp;&nbsp;
About half the country had them before, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:30.480 --> 01:02:38.000
weren't necessarily all age.&nbsp; So thank you to&nbsp;
colleagues.&nbsp; And I would really wish to extend&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:38.000 --> 01:02:42.640
my thanks to colleagues in the sector because&nbsp;
they have not stopped.&nbsp; They're very focused&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:44.000 --> 01:02:50.400
on delivering our plan.&nbsp; Those crisis lines, at&nbsp;
least 70% of them are currently linked in with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:51.120 --> 01:02:58.560
the 111 services so that 111 can hot-patch&nbsp;
patients through to their local crisis services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:58.560 --> 01:03:04.400
and the remaining ones are in the throes&nbsp;
of being linked to 111.&nbsp; So we will very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:04.400 --> 01:03:10.640
soon have a 111 specialist mental&nbsp;
health 24/7 crisis service in place.&nbsp;

01:03:11.280 --> 01:03:17.840
We hadn't expected in the long-term plan to be&nbsp;
setting up staff hubs for confidential assessment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:17.840 --> 01:03:23.840
and treatment of staff mental health across the&nbsp;
NHS.&nbsp; And we did that all parts of the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:23.840 --> 01:03:30.320
have those hubs.&nbsp; They've been accessed&nbsp;
thousands of times by colleagues from across&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:30.320 --> 01:03:36.160
the NHS.&nbsp; They're not there for mental health&nbsp;
staff alone, they're there for everybody.&nbsp; And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:36.160 --> 01:03:42.640
so we established those hubs and we really kept&nbsp;
all of our services going one way or another.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:43.840 --> 01:03:49.280
And particularly, we lent on digital platforms&nbsp;
and applications to see patients.&nbsp; And I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:49.280 --> 01:03:52.800
going to ask Professor Kendall to say, Tim&nbsp;
to say something about that in a moment.&nbsp;

01:03:53.840 --> 01:04:00.160
And the other things we did where we didn't stop&nbsp;
was we do believe that provider collaboratives&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:00.160 --> 01:04:07.280
will be a very important part of both the present,&nbsp;
but also the future as we introduce ICSs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:08.160 --> 01:04:14.320
integrated services at place.&nbsp; We think that&nbsp;
provider collaboratives will have a great deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:14.320 --> 01:04:19.600
to offer there.&nbsp; So we carried on rolling out&nbsp;
provider collaboratives across the country.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:19.600 --> 01:04:25.680
Now all parts of the country are covered.&nbsp; There&nbsp;
are just a few more to bring live over the coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:25.680 --> 01:04:31.520
12 months.&nbsp; And we think that's going to be an&nbsp;
important capability to improve both quality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:31.520 --> 01:04:37.920
but also change the model of care.&nbsp; And those&nbsp;
NHS-led collaboratives are absolutely charged&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:37.920 --> 01:04:42.720
to tackle inequalities, work with partners&nbsp;
and local communities as well.&nbsp; And we put&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:42.720 --> 01:04:47.040
just a few examples in the appendices of some&nbsp;
really good work they were already achieving.&nbsp;

01:04:48.080 --> 01:04:52.640
And I was also delighted that we&nbsp;
published our advancing inequalities&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:53.760 --> 01:04:56.960
strategy for mental health, or advancing equality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:57.840 --> 01:05:01.440
back last October, and we've done a&nbsp;
great deal of work in that space too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:02.160 --> 01:05:12.080
So, many of our pathways and our&nbsp;
programmes continue to deliver and be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:12.640 --> 01:05:19.760
close to or on trajectory.&nbsp; So if we think back&nbsp;
to the long-term plan promises, are we on track?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:19.760 --> 01:05:25.360
Many of them are in addition to that work that&nbsp;
I've just mentioned.&nbsp; Others have been affected&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:26.640 --> 01:05:34.640
more considerably.&nbsp; So for example, we saw a real&nbsp;
tail off during the first waves of COVID of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:34.640 --> 01:05:39.920
coming forward for talking therapies.&nbsp; Even though&nbsp;
they were open access, we moved them online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:40.800 --> 01:05:46.320
that dropped off.&nbsp; That's very typical&nbsp;
internationally when a major crisis strikes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:46.320 --> 01:05:51.280
but of course, what happens is the need doesn't&nbsp;
go away.&nbsp; You have pent-up demand, and we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:51.280 --> 01:05:58.320
now seeing huge demand and we're back to pre-COVID&nbsp;
level, and we're working very hard to expand those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:58.320 --> 01:06:04.640
services.&nbsp; We also continue to roll out mental&nbsp;
health support teams in schools, and indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:04.640 --> 01:06:10.000
we've been fast-tracking their roll outs.&nbsp;&nbsp;
And happy to answer questions on that as well.&nbsp;

01:06:11.280 --> 01:06:15.920
Over the next three to six months, right now,&nbsp;
we're obviously focused along with the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:15.920 --> 01:06:24.880
the NHS on our urgent and emergency care pathways,&nbsp;
working with A&amp;E ambulance services and others to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:24.880 --> 01:06:31.360
make sure that the patient experience for mental&nbsp;
health patients is a good one, is a safe one.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:31.360 --> 01:06:38.800
And certainly we've invested an additional&nbsp;
£20&nbsp;million over this period for colleagues&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:38.800 --> 01:06:44.080
to do things, for example, like put mental&nbsp;
health experts in ambulance control rooms&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:44.080 --> 01:06:50.480
and in 111.&nbsp; We're getting some great feedback&nbsp;
there.&nbsp; We're diverting people from A&amp;E to home&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:50.480 --> 01:06:56.640
treatment and intensive support there.&nbsp; And of&nbsp;
course, the crisis lines I mentioned earlier.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:56.640 --> 01:07:01.360
So we're really focusing working with Pauline&nbsp;
Phillip and her team on the winter plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:02.560 --> 01:07:11.520
but restoring the long-term plan trajectories&nbsp;
as fast as we can.&nbsp; Focusing also on physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:11.520 --> 01:07:17.040
health checks for people with mental illness&nbsp;
and really doubling down on that inequality.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:17.680 --> 01:07:21.360
Making sure that mental health is&nbsp;
positioned correctly in the ICSs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:22.160 --> 01:07:28.720
making sure we deliver on our promises, are really&nbsp;
– we're in full stride, let's just say that.&nbsp;

01:07:28.720 --> 01:07:36.800
We've got a very creative sector.&nbsp; We've recruited&nbsp;
an additional 18,000 staff thus far.&nbsp; And many&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:36.800 --> 01:07:42.000
thanks to colleagues at Health Education England&nbsp;
across the country because we've developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:42.000 --> 01:07:49.120
new roles, apprenticeships, the psychological&nbsp;
practitioners in IAPT, in schools and elsewhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:49.120 --> 01:07:55.040
We're looking at a whole range of other new&nbsp;
roles as well.&nbsp; So workforce is both our biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:55.040 --> 01:08:03.120
opportunity and our biggest challenge.&nbsp; We need&nbsp;
27,000 more staff by 2023-24 to power or plan.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:03.760 --> 01:08:13.760
So we want to tackle our waiting lists, our own&nbsp;
form of elective waiting lists.&nbsp; Steady our UEC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:13.760 --> 01:08:19.520
our Urgent Emergency Care pathways.&nbsp; Continue&nbsp;
to support staff, continue to roll out provider&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:19.520 --> 01:08:27.680
collaboratives and restore the LTP trajectories.&nbsp;&nbsp;
I have to say the secretary is so engaged and up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:27.680 --> 01:08:32.960
for this as our charity colleagues.&nbsp; I'd like to&nbsp;
thank them all.&nbsp; But I wondered whether I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:32.960 --> 01:08:39.200
just hand to Tim briefly to talk about how did we&nbsp;
keep services going digitally?&nbsp; Where are we going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:39.200 --> 01:08:46.400
next?&nbsp; And what innovations do we see there, Tim?
&gt;&gt;Tim Kendall: Sure.&nbsp; I think the first thing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:47.200 --> 01:08:56.320
say is we were very surprised in areas like IAPT&nbsp;
and in other parts of mental health that very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:56.320 --> 01:09:03.760
quickly moving on to using telephones and videos&nbsp;
and so on to maintain contact with patients.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:05.120 --> 01:09:08.800
And there is a growing evidence&nbsp;
base that actually if you deliver&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:09.920 --> 01:09:15.760
therapies by telephone, that's been over some&nbsp;
time, but also by video, that you actually get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:16.480 --> 01:09:22.000
comparable sorts of results to face-to-face.&nbsp; So I&nbsp;
think where we've ended up with this is that yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:22.000 --> 01:09:30.400
we are expanding access to video and telephone&nbsp;
therapies, but it's basically about choice now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:30.400 --> 01:09:38.480
is what do people prefer?&nbsp; So if you're a&nbsp;
young person with autism, you are very likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:38.480 --> 01:09:48.960
to prefer doing this by video.&nbsp; And we've got way&nbsp;
better results on DNAs, on people not attending.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:51.040 --> 01:09:58.400
Instead, lots of kids with autism will now&nbsp;
attend probably 100%.&nbsp; So that's all good news.&nbsp;

01:09:58.400 --> 01:10:07.680
Now, it has brought us back to looking at where&nbsp;
can we innovate more in this because we've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:07.680 --> 01:10:14.400
some good evidence, but it's not good enough,&nbsp;
around what we'd call blended therapies, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:14.400 --> 01:10:23.520
you can do your therapies by video but you can&nbsp;
also do it face to face.&nbsp; And you can mix that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:23.520 --> 01:10:30.720
with a digitally, sort of a digitally-enabled&nbsp;
therapy.&nbsp; So you actually see your therapist&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:30.720 --> 01:10:36.080
maybe four times, and between times you have&nbsp;
a programme of therapy that you work through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:36.080 --> 01:10:43.600
on your computer.&nbsp; And there are – there is&nbsp;
evidence that these are quite good.&nbsp; But problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:43.600 --> 01:10:49.920
is that where there is evidence that they're good,&nbsp;
they are technologically very unsophisticated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:50.800 --> 01:10:57.280
which probably means people won't use them, or&nbsp;
if they are technologically very sophisticated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:57.280 --> 01:11:03.120
there's not much evidence that they're&nbsp;
effective.&nbsp; So this will feed into David's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:03.840 --> 01:11:09.760
hope for a life science programme stimulating&nbsp;
innovative research in mental health.&nbsp;

01:11:11.280 --> 01:11:17.600
There is lots going on and we've got stuff&nbsp;
recently that's been landed on my desk.&nbsp; A&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:17.600 --> 01:11:22.080
really good piece of work done&nbsp;
at MindTech in Nottingham around&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:24.480 --> 01:11:33.200
digital interventions for children with tics.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Really big problem in kids.&nbsp; And the question is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:33.200 --> 01:11:40.400
how can we scale this up quickly?&nbsp; How can we get&nbsp;
this into the NHS?&nbsp; And another one on developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:41.040 --> 01:11:47.680
digital algorithm-based questionnaires that&nbsp;
you apply digitally that could give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:48.560 --> 01:11:52.560
what you might think of as a sort&nbsp;
of digital thermometer for mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:52.560 --> 01:11:57.280
health.&nbsp; Is someone actually suffering from&nbsp;
a mental health problem or not?&nbsp; And yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:58.240 --> 01:12:04.160
this could go into a good research programme.&nbsp;&nbsp;
It could be incredibly useful in areas like A&amp;E.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:05.200 --> 01:12:12.800
So very exciting that we're in the foothills of&nbsp;
this, stimulated very much by our experience in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:12.800 --> 01:12:17.520
COVID but still quite a way to go.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:17.520 --> 01:12:23.200
Tim, I wonder whether you could just give the&nbsp;
board an impression of the – obviously anonymised,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:23.200 --> 01:12:30.240
of the kind of patients – homeless patients that&nbsp;
you're working with in Sheffield.&nbsp; Just to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:30.240 --> 01:12:36.400
us a feel for some of the difficulties that&nbsp;
you encounter in your sort of clinical life.&nbsp;

01:12:36.400 --> 01:12:43.920
&gt;&gt;Tim Kendall: Yeah, of course.&nbsp; Quite a lot&nbsp;
have what you'd call multiple co-morbidities&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:43.920 --> 01:12:50.640
or multiple morbidity.&nbsp; So – and we look at that&nbsp;
in terms of people with mental health problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:50.640 --> 01:12:56.960
schizophrenia, bipolar, personality&nbsp;
disorder, et cetera.&nbsp; Those with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:58.320 --> 01:13:06.320
serious addictions, usually alcohol and/or drugs,&nbsp;
heroin quite commonly.&nbsp; And then on top of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:06.320 --> 01:13:11.360
those with physical health problems.&nbsp; So that's&nbsp;
a really common combination that they've got all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:11.360 --> 01:13:20.000
three.&nbsp; And they might also use prisons a lot.&nbsp;
So one I saw last week.&nbsp; Young man came out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:20.000 --> 01:13:30.000
Afghanistan, 14 years old, five years ago.&nbsp; Took&nbsp;
him two years to get to England.&nbsp; He's now got&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:35.440
quite a significant mental health problem.&nbsp; I&nbsp;
think he's also got some neurological problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:36.480 --> 01:13:44.160
and he doesn't have an addiction but&nbsp;
he's very damaged by his experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:44.160 --> 01:13:50.000
and has no home.&nbsp; So yeah, obviously our first&nbsp;
port of call is to get him somewhere to live.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:51.120 --> 01:13:55.760
But then you've got the physical health problems&nbsp;
and mental health problems to deal with.&nbsp;

01:13:56.480 --> 01:14:03.200
Others might be people who have lost their&nbsp;
home following the collapse of a business.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:04.160 --> 01:14:10.000
So someone with a printing business where&nbsp;
they're operating on relatively small margins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:10.560 --> 01:14:16.800
and they have a marital breakdown, turn to&nbsp;
drinking and before you know it, they've lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:16.800 --> 01:14:22.560
their house, their business and everything,&nbsp;
they're on the street.&nbsp; Very, very depressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:22.560 --> 01:14:32.080
and ready to kill themselves.&nbsp; So it's quite a&nbsp;
range from people, you know – I could go on, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:33.200 --> 01:14:37.440
it would take most of the rest of the meeting.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: No, I think it was really helpful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:38.000 --> 01:14:44.720
Tim, because when we talk about the level of unmet&nbsp;
need in mental health, it's a level that would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:44.720 --> 01:14:51.600
not tolerated in any other illness.&nbsp; I mean, I&nbsp;
don't know how you'd assess the level of unmet&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:51.600 --> 01:14:59.680
need, but there are a lot of very fragile,&nbsp;
very ill people for whom the help we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:59.680 --> 01:15:04.121
is very limited and very hard&nbsp;
for them to access for all the –&nbsp;

01:15:04.121 --> 01:15:09.200
&gt;&gt;Tim Kendall: I absolutely agree with you.&nbsp;&nbsp;
I mean, we can actually quantify it.&nbsp; I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:09.200 --> 01:15:18.480
in 2016, Claire will echo this loudly, I think,&nbsp;
2016, we were seeing one out of four children&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:18.480 --> 01:15:24.720
that had a mental health problem.&nbsp; And the one&nbsp;
out of four that was getting treatment wasn't to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:24.720 --> 01:15:31.920
do with severity.&nbsp; It was to do with the ability&nbsp;
of parents to access services more than anything.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:32.560 --> 01:15:41.920
So we've now changed that to about 40% of kids&nbsp;
being seen.&nbsp; So we will get there in the end, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:41.920 --> 01:15:48.800
it takes huge amounts of time of training staff&nbsp;
and getting the investment in the right place.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:49.920 --> 01:15:56.080
The equivalent numbers for adults is that it&nbsp;
was just under 40% when we started and we think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:56.080 --> 01:16:00.880
we're not so clear on this, we think we've&nbsp;
probably getting up to just over 50%.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:01.840 --> 01:16:08.960
But it's – it wouldn't be tolerable if&nbsp;
you say we're only seeing one out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:08.960 --> 01:16:18.400
– 40% of people with cancer, you'd be –&nbsp;
we'd get lynched understandably.&nbsp; Yes.&nbsp;

01:16:18.400 --> 01:16:22.400
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: And just to say, we've done&nbsp;
some modelling on children and young people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:24.240 --> 01:16:29.840
which makes us think that we can have closed&nbsp;
that treatment gap to an acceptable level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:29.840 --> 01:16:39.440
we've done the modelling and looked at the&nbsp;
evidence base, by 2027, 2028.&nbsp; And we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:39.440 --> 01:16:43.440
the sorts of services that we need to roll&nbsp;
out, including the mental health support teams&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:43.440 --> 01:16:50.480
in schools everywhere, not just in 40% of the&nbsp;
country, which is where we will be by 2023-24.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:51.120 --> 01:16:56.640
So as Tim says, I think there's often a&nbsp;
temptation with mental health because it's such&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:56.640 --> 01:17:04.560
a big systemic underserved set of patients and&nbsp;
populations, there's temptations to think we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:04.560 --> 01:17:10.640
never close that gap or close it to an acceptable&nbsp;
level.&nbsp; We absolutely can.&nbsp; And our programme&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:11.600 --> 01:17:16.960
is demonstrating that's possible.&nbsp; We're&nbsp;
not wanting to pathologise, medicalise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:16.960 --> 01:17:23.440
or distress, which is why we work closely&nbsp;
with schools and charities and communities&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:23.440 --> 01:17:30.800
and put self-help apps and things like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;
But in terms of those who have the most severe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:30.800 --> 01:17:36.400
illnesses or the most treatable, it is possible&nbsp;
to close that gap, and we absolutely should.&nbsp;

01:17:36.400 --> 01:17:42.400
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Claire.&nbsp; Wol.
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Thanks, David.&nbsp; I mean, Claire, Tim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:42.400 --> 01:17:47.120
thanks for your presentation.&nbsp; It's such an&nbsp;
important area which deserves, as you rightly say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:47.120 --> 01:17:54.880
focus.&nbsp; A couple of questions from me.&nbsp; The first&nbsp;
is, when you talk about working with people, being&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:54.880 --> 01:18:00.000
aware of the landscape, what's going on.&nbsp; So in&nbsp;
my previous life, I chaired the Guy's &amp; St Thomas'&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:00.640 --> 01:18:06.640
Foundation.&nbsp; And there, one of their programmes is&nbsp;
on adolescent mental health.&nbsp; And just connecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:06.640 --> 01:18:14.400
the dots because they're looking at the&nbsp;
space, what's going on and how to fund&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:14.400 --> 01:18:19.520
ways of making these issues sort of be reduced&nbsp;
in due course.&nbsp; But also one of the things they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:19.520 --> 01:18:26.560
do – and my firm, my own business joined up&nbsp;
with the charity in funding well-being zones in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:26.560 --> 01:18:31.520
hospitals.&nbsp; And we had an amazing presentation&nbsp;
from a doctor, a psychologist, Dr Neil Rees.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:32.560 --> 01:18:38.160
And he was talking about the fact that the huge&nbsp;
benefit this has been to the pandemic for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:38.160 --> 01:18:43.680
workforce.&nbsp; And I think I'm right in saying, he&nbsp;
said, actually, it's not being funded across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:43.680 --> 01:18:49.040
system.&nbsp; As in that it felt like a one-off.&nbsp; I&nbsp;
could have this completely wrong, in which case&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:49.040 --> 01:18:54.400
I shall go back in my box.&nbsp; But it just struck&nbsp;
me that, one, it took outside money to fund it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:54.400 --> 01:18:59.360
which is a bit odd.&nbsp; Delighted to, but it's&nbsp;
a bit odd.&nbsp; Two, having shown it works really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:59.360 --> 01:19:04.240
well, why not run it everywhere else?
&gt;&gt;Tim Kendall:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:04.240 --> 01:19:09.120
What was the intervention, Wol?
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: So, well-being zones.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:10.960 --> 01:19:15.520
Comprehensive, psychological and spiritual&nbsp;
support for the staff.&nbsp; And the key, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:15.520 --> 01:19:22.720
is having a trained psychologist in the situation&nbsp;
working for the workforce, as opposed to patients.&nbsp;

01:19:22.720 --> 01:19:31.600
&gt;&gt;Tim Kendall: No, the – many of these things work&nbsp;
phenomenally well, but for us to get this rolled&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:31.600 --> 01:19:36.480
out nationally, we've got to have randomised&nbsp;
controlled trial evidence for it or at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:37.200 --> 01:19:46.160
pretty decent evidence that would work.&nbsp; Now, as&nbsp;
far as adolescents are concerned, crying out for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:46.160 --> 01:19:51.600
help in this area.&nbsp; During the pandemic, they have&nbsp;
suffered more than any other group in my view.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:53.040 --> 01:19:59.200
We've gone up from probably one in nine having a&nbsp;
probable mental health problem up to one in six.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:00.240 --> 01:20:10.240
And the group that probably have been most&nbsp;
affected are young women.&nbsp; So 16 to 25-year-oldss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:11.600 --> 01:20:18.080
27% of women have now got a probable&nbsp;
mental disorder.&nbsp; And that's twice the rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:18.080 --> 01:20:28.800
of men in that group.&nbsp; So huge need.&nbsp; We do have&nbsp;
interventions for this group, but we need a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:28.800 --> 01:20:34.240
more research around which work and which don't.
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: And my third point, sorry, David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:34.240 --> 01:20:41.760
to jump in.&nbsp; Actually it's offline, I apologise.&nbsp;&nbsp;
But there is a particular philanthropist&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:41.760 --> 01:20:48.320
who has a vast sum of money, whose basic goal is&nbsp;
to cure mental health.&nbsp; I mean, extraordinary.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:49.680 --> 01:20:51.360
But it's connecting up with people like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:52.400 --> 01:20:55.360
which I can help you do, by the way.
&gt;&gt;Tim Kendall: We would be delighted.&nbsp;

01:20:55.360 --> 01:20:59.920
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Perhaps I can take it offline.
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: I would also just add on staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:59.920 --> 01:21:08.400
if I might, that I think throughout this&nbsp;
period, I have never seen organisations&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:09.120 --> 01:21:13.200
offer so much.&nbsp; Whether that's&nbsp;
enhancing their occupational health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:13.200 --> 01:21:20.000
looking at other well-being offers, working&nbsp;
with the hospital padre, other faith leaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:20.000 --> 01:21:26.320
other community resources for staff.&nbsp; The&nbsp;
mental health hubs that I mentioned earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:27.520 --> 01:21:33.680
are really, if I think of it as a pyramid, is&nbsp;
at the top of that pyramid where all of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:33.680 --> 01:21:42.640
other interventions, somebody's still suffering&nbsp;
to a degree where their life is being affected.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:42.640 --> 01:21:49.360
And we wanted to, as an NHS, ensure that we could&nbsp;
fast-track assessment and confidential treatment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:49.360 --> 01:21:54.640
where a colleague needed it.&nbsp; And these hubs have&nbsp;
been quite incredible, but they can't replace&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:54.640 --> 01:22:00.880
and shouldn't be, instead of those well-being&nbsp;
offers.&nbsp; And we've had to mobilise at such speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:00.880 --> 01:22:06.320
and I'm so impressed with what colleagues have&nbsp;
done across the country across the sectors.&nbsp;

01:22:06.320 --> 01:22:12.080
But I think you raised an important point of&nbsp;
how we learn from the best of those schemes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:12.080 --> 01:22:17.280
because I am hoping, and I think many of us&nbsp;
are, that a permanent legacy of COVID will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:19.120 --> 01:22:24.160
a more robust offer around staff support&nbsp;
and staff well-being.&nbsp; Not necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:24.160 --> 01:22:28.720
mental health assessment and treatment&nbsp;
alone, it's all those other things.&nbsp; So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:28.720 --> 01:22:36.400
I'm sure Prerana and others will want to look&nbsp;
at how we share the best practice and what we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:36.400 --> 01:22:40.880
learned during this period because some quite&nbsp;
incredible things have been put in place for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:40.880 --> 01:22:46.560
colleagues over this time.&nbsp; And what you've done,&nbsp;
I'd love to learn more about that as well and get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:46.560 --> 01:22:51.920
the evidence.&nbsp; We should take that offline
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And, Wol, just so you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:51.920 --> 01:22:56.480
we've got a pretty significant financial&nbsp;
commitment from the government and from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:56.480 --> 01:23:02.640
industry to do – address exactly the point that&nbsp;
your donor friend is also addressing.&nbsp; You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:02.640 --> 01:23:08.240
how do we get new innovative therapeutic,&nbsp;
digital and pharmaceutical out there to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:08.240 --> 01:23:12.560
address some of these issues.&nbsp; So maybe we can&nbsp;
pick that up offline as well.&nbsp; Andrew Morris.&nbsp;

01:23:12.560 --> 01:23:19.520
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: Thanks, David.&nbsp; I just want to&nbsp;
pay tribute to the mental health liaison service&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:19.520 --> 01:23:24.320
and the progress you've made with that, Claire&nbsp;
and Tim.&nbsp; It's such an important service.&nbsp; It&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:24.320 --> 01:23:30.400
does make so much difference to the patients who&nbsp;
attend A&amp;E with a mental health problem.&nbsp; And it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:30.400 --> 01:23:35.920
makes a big difference to the staff in A&amp;E who've&nbsp;
got the reassurance and the right level of support&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:35.920 --> 01:23:43.600
to treat people.&nbsp; My only slight point or issue&nbsp;
is around workforce because I always thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:43.600 --> 01:23:51.200
the rate limiting factor was not having enough&nbsp;
people to give 100% cover.&nbsp; And I'd just be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:51.200 --> 01:23:56.240
grateful for your kind of reassurance on this&nbsp;
because I see the aspiration is to get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:56.240 --> 01:24:02.320
100% of all trusts providing a 24/7 service&nbsp;
within this financial year.&nbsp; So it'd be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:02.960 --> 01:24:06.160
just good to hear your take on it.
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Yes.&nbsp; Well, firstly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:06.160 --> 01:24:11.840
I'll go first and then perhaps hand over to&nbsp;
Tim.&nbsp; Firstly, thank you, Andrew.&nbsp; There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:11.840 --> 01:24:19.360
many aspects of mental health provision that exist&nbsp;
in our country now that didn't exist universally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:19.360 --> 01:24:25.440
three or four years ago.&nbsp; Children's eating&nbsp;
disorder services, we didn't really have any,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:25.440 --> 01:24:30.320
now all parts of the country do.&nbsp; Now they're too&nbsp;
busy and we want to grow them, but we've got them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:30.320 --> 01:24:35.760
in all parts of the country.&nbsp; The specialist&nbsp;
perinatal services for high risk women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:35.760 --> 01:24:40.880
we didn't have them everywhere, we had them in 15%&nbsp;
of the country, now we have them everywhere.&nbsp; The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:40.880 --> 01:24:47.680
IAPT programme is the envy of the world actually,&nbsp;
and so on and so forth.&nbsp; And of course the liaison&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:47.680 --> 01:24:54.000
services are a really good example of where they&nbsp;
existed in pockets before our programme began.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:54.560 --> 01:25:01.600
And now we do have a liaison service in all&nbsp;
major acutes.&nbsp; And something like, the latest is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:01.600 --> 01:25:09.280
something like 70%, 75% of them are core 24, and&nbsp;
we're determined to make that 100% of them.&nbsp; So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:09.280 --> 01:25:16.080
it is amazing that there's this quiet revolution&nbsp;
happening, notwithstanding that Lord Prior's point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:16.080 --> 01:25:20.080
about there's still this treatment gap that we&nbsp;
mustn't rest on our laurels till we've closed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:21.280 --> 01:25:30.160
Yes, look, the workforce is our single biggest&nbsp;
opportunity and rate limiting factor.&nbsp; It's great&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:30.160 --> 01:25:35.680
that we've seen more nurses coming into training.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Thank you very much, Ruth May and colleagues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:36.480 --> 01:25:41.840
because we've seen an uptick in mental health&nbsp;
nurses coming into training.&nbsp; As you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:41.840 --> 01:25:47.840
they take three years to train.&nbsp; And we are&nbsp;
asking organisations to look really hard at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:47.840 --> 01:25:53.440
their retention, that whole retirement return&nbsp;
and everything else.&nbsp; But – and we're also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:53.440 --> 01:26:00.000
looking at new roles, including in A&amp;E and acute&nbsp;
hospitals because these teams aren't just in A&amp;E,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:00.000 --> 01:26:06.240
they go up onto the wards.&nbsp; But I would say that&nbsp;
is one of the things that colleagues will say&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:06.880 --> 01:26:11.120
is their biggest challenge.&nbsp; But we're&nbsp;
creating new roles.&nbsp; We've seen an uptick&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:11.120 --> 01:26:16.800
in people coming in to training.&nbsp; We've got to&nbsp;
double down on retention as well.&nbsp; And, Tim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:16.800 --> 01:26:21.040
I don't know if there's anything else you would&nbsp;
like to add, particularly on the psychiatry front.&nbsp;

01:26:21.040 --> 01:26:29.760
&gt;&gt;Tim Kendall: Just the work that liaison&nbsp;
teams do within acute hospitals.&nbsp; There's some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:29.760 --> 01:26:38.080
which they can easily hand on to IAPT services.&nbsp;&nbsp;
So people with depression and anxiety and so on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:38.080 --> 01:26:43.360
that's fairly straightforward.&nbsp; But also people&nbsp;
with physical health problems, who've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:43.360 --> 01:26:50.560
associated anxiety and depression and so on,&nbsp;
now they can go into IAPT.&nbsp; And we've got some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:50.560 --> 01:26:55.440
really good evidence that was published earlier&nbsp;
this year, where it shows that for every pound&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:55.440 --> 01:27:00.880
you spend on the psychological health for&nbsp;
people with chronic physical health conditions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:00.880 --> 01:27:07.200
you save £2 for the rest of the NHS.&nbsp; So&nbsp;
it's a really, really important area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:07.200 --> 01:27:14.960
But the other group of people that we see&nbsp;
in liaison really require a complex response&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:15.520 --> 01:27:22.720
and they usually need psychiatrists.&nbsp; And that&nbsp;
is a bit of a limiting factor at the moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:26.160 --> 01:27:31.360
We're full, we've got all our posts, I think,&nbsp;
pretty much full at consultant level, but we need&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:31.360 --> 01:27:41.680
to expand them more.&nbsp; And I think we will struggle&nbsp;
to get a full set of consultants in A&amp;E by 2027&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:41.680 --> 01:27:46.480
across the board.&nbsp; That will – it'll stretch us.
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: I think the other thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:46.480 --> 01:27:52.960
I would say is, sorry, just one last point.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Bringing certainty around workforce planning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:53.680 --> 01:27:56.800
which very much rests on&nbsp;
certainty around investment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:57.680 --> 01:28:04.800
has been an incredibly important part of&nbsp;
our programme to date.&nbsp; So three years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:04.800 --> 01:28:10.560
we said what each year's trajectory would&nbsp;
be across all of the programmes.&nbsp; And we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:10.560 --> 01:28:16.240
been really actively working with HES since to&nbsp;
make sure that we're doing as much proactive&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:16.240 --> 01:28:22.160
planning and new role development as we can.&nbsp; But&nbsp;
it's our biggest challenge.&nbsp; No doubt about it.&nbsp;

01:28:22.160 --> 01:28:26.240
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ruth.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Very briefly to build on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:26.800 --> 01:28:34.320
comments about nursing workforce.&nbsp; So normally we&nbsp;
would train 3,500 student nurses a year in mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:34.320 --> 01:28:41.360
health.&nbsp; This last year, we've started with&nbsp;
4,800.&nbsp; That's a big increase.&nbsp; Now they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:41.360 --> 01:28:47.600
not going to be ready for another three years, but&nbsp;
there's a great story to tell about a pipeline.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:47.600 --> 01:28:52.800
So thank you to Mark Radford and HE colleagues&nbsp;
that have supported that.&nbsp; And of course, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:52.800 --> 01:29:00.160
Claire will know that we have – building on the&nbsp;
international nurse recruitment looking to support&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:00.160 --> 01:29:06.480
international nurse recruitment with nurses with&nbsp;
a mental health skill and experience.&nbsp; So that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:06.480 --> 01:29:13.200
hope will start to support Claire and the team.
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: And there's never been such&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:13.200 --> 01:29:19.280
interest in working in mental health.&nbsp; So I think&nbsp;
COVID especially has piqued everybody's interest.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:19.280 --> 01:29:25.600
Society is talking more openly than it did&nbsp;
about the impacts of, in this case COVID, but on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:25.600 --> 01:29:32.080
people's mental health.&nbsp; And I think it absolutely&nbsp;
behoves us over the next year two and three&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:32.720 --> 01:29:38.400
to really draw on that interest, to bring people&nbsp;
in through apprenticeship routes, which aren't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:38.400 --> 01:29:44.480
for example, which are not just for the more&nbsp;
lowly-graded posts or more entry-level posts.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:44.480 --> 01:29:50.560
You can have apprenticeships at degree level&nbsp;
or masters level.&nbsp; So I think we really need&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:50.560 --> 01:29:56.240
to take this opportunity.&nbsp; And I know provider&nbsp;
colleagues across the country and others are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:56.240 --> 01:30:03.520
doing some phenomenal work looking at how we&nbsp;
draw on local communities, different routes into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:03.520 --> 01:30:09.120
psychiatry and mental health as well as through&nbsp;
the more traditional routes and training our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:09.120 --> 01:30:15.280
doctors, nurses, therapists, OTs, et cetera.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:15.280 --> 01:30:19.840
And Tim, thanks very much.&nbsp; Tim Kendall,&nbsp;
that is.&nbsp; Thank you both very much for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:19.840 --> 01:30:25.840
joining us.&nbsp; And the work you do is incredibly&nbsp;
important.&nbsp; Keep at it.&nbsp; Thank you very much.&nbsp;

01:30:25.840 --> 01:30:27.040
&gt;&gt;Tim Kendall:&nbsp; Thanks, David.
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Thank you.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:27.040 --> 01:30:29.440
It's a privilege.&nbsp; Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks.&nbsp; Thanks.&nbsp; Bye.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:30.400 --> 01:30:36.320
So moving on to the next item, which is maternity&nbsp;
and neonate services.&nbsp; Ruth, I think perhaps you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:36.320 --> 01:30:42.080
could introduce Jacqui and Aidan.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Yeah, thanks very much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:43.040 --> 01:30:49.600
David and colleagues.&nbsp; This is an opportunity&nbsp;
to talk through the progress we've been making&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:49.600 --> 01:30:55.360
since the last time we were in front of&nbsp;
the public board in March 2021.&nbsp; Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:55.360 --> 01:31:02.880
on the 9th of December will be the first year&nbsp;
of Donna Ockenden's report into the care at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:02.880 --> 01:31:08.400
Shrewsbury and Telford.&nbsp; So, first off, I'm&nbsp;
going to ask Aidan to give you an update on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:08.400 --> 01:31:14.800
the review and the others too.&nbsp; And then I shall&nbsp;
kick off with the main body of the report.&nbsp; Aidan.&nbsp;

01:31:14.800 --> 01:31:20.480
&gt;&gt;Aidan Fowler: Thanks, Ruth.&nbsp; So there are three&nbsp;
reviews that you'll see outlined in the paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:21.120 --> 01:31:28.160
Firstly in East Kent.&nbsp; That's&nbsp;
underway with Bill Kirkup as chair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:28.160 --> 01:31:34.720
and we're expecting to see a report from that&nbsp;
in the sort of second half of next year.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:35.280 --> 01:31:40.240
The second report to mention is in Nottingham&nbsp;
University Trust into their maternity unit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:40.240 --> 01:31:46.560
which is a regionally commissioned review that we&nbsp;
have worked with them on.&nbsp; That is now underway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:46.560 --> 01:31:52.880
but it's likely not to report until 2023.&nbsp;
Just to update on the Ockenden review.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:52.880 --> 01:32:01.120
As you know, this is much the largest review and&nbsp;
they have considered 1,862 cases, not all of which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:01.120 --> 01:32:08.880
I think will be included but the majority of which&nbsp;
will, and had been expecting to produce their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:08.880 --> 01:32:13.040
second and final report by the end of this year,&nbsp;
but have asked for an extension.&nbsp; There were some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:14.240 --> 01:32:18.400
further papers forthcoming from the trust&nbsp;
and there was a very high volume of cases.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:18.960 --> 01:32:26.000
So they have – they wrote to the Secretary&nbsp;
of State and had a letter back this week from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:26.000 --> 01:32:33.120
one of the ministers, Maria Caulfield, to accept&nbsp;
that they will publish the report either on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:33.120 --> 01:32:40.400
or potentially slightly before for logistic&nbsp;
reasons the 24th of March next year.&nbsp; And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:41.200 --> 01:32:49.120
they are still meeting with the minister to agree&nbsp;
processes to follow that for feedback and so on.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:49.120 --> 01:32:53.680
But we would expect that report, therefore,&nbsp;
next year, which will be reporting into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:53.680 --> 01:33:02.240
very significant number of cases in that trust.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you, Aidan.&nbsp; So you'll recall&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:02.240 --> 01:33:07.840
that the board agreed at its meeting in March&nbsp;
earlier this year that we would have £95.6&nbsp;million&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:09.120 --> 01:33:14.880
mainly for midwifery staff, obstetric staff, and&nbsp;
then of course, emergency disciplinary training.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:15.600 --> 01:33:22.000
I'm pleased to say that with midwifery staff,&nbsp;
first we will talk about, pleased to say that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:22.000 --> 01:33:29.680
said that we wanted 1,200 new midwifery posts and&nbsp;
indeed those have – trusts have put those posts&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:29.680 --> 01:33:36.240
into their establishments, which I'm pleased to&nbsp;
see.&nbsp; Of course, we had 600 midwifery vacancies&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:36.240 --> 01:33:44.800
at the start of this before Ockenden reported.&nbsp;&nbsp;
And so the challenge has been, how do we recruit&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:44.800 --> 01:33:52.880
to these midwifery posts?&nbsp; I'm pleased to say that&nbsp;
already we have got the 500 new student midwives&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:54.240 --> 01:33:59.520
off the – onto the register.&nbsp; And so that&nbsp;
is starting to be making a difference.&nbsp;

01:34:00.160 --> 01:34:06.240
Also the international recruitment, we&nbsp;
said that we were going to deliver 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:06.880 --> 01:34:13.680
new international midwives.&nbsp; We've&nbsp;
already got 400 in the interview process.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:13.680 --> 01:34:20.560
So much work to do.&nbsp; But one of the things that&nbsp;
my ambition is, is to ensure that by the time we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:20.560 --> 01:34:27.600
get to the end of this financial year, we would&nbsp;
have recruited those 1,200 midwives and more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:28.880 --> 01:34:34.560
With regard to obstetricians, it's slightly more&nbsp;
complex because we actually at the moment don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:34.560 --> 01:34:44.080
record separately the obstetrics and gynaecology&nbsp;
work consultants.&nbsp; We record them as one on our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:44.080 --> 01:34:49.520
ESR.&nbsp; So we're doing a lot of work with our trust&nbsp;
in terms of the data collection so we've got that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:49.520 --> 01:34:58.400
granularity of detail of their split of time.&nbsp; I&nbsp;
am confident that we will work towards this, but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:58.400 --> 01:35:08.400
have yet to see the confidence by trusts about&nbsp;
whether we have a future obstetric supply.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:09.120 --> 01:35:14.080
We are working extremely well, though, with the&nbsp;
Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:14.080 --> 01:35:20.800
and they were being funded by the DHSC to&nbsp;
develop a comprehensive obstetric work plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:20.800 --> 01:35:25.440
and model.&nbsp; And I'm pleased to say&nbsp;
that that is progressing very well.&nbsp;

01:35:27.280 --> 01:35:35.040
We – you'll remember that we asked trusts in our&nbsp;
letter on the 14th of December last year to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:35.040 --> 01:35:44.560
through Donna Ockenden's immediate essential&nbsp;
actions.&nbsp; I'm asking the board today to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:44.560 --> 01:35:52.640
sure that we ask our trusts again to take to their&nbsp;
board in public, in quarter four, their progress&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:52.640 --> 01:35:59.520
against those actions.&nbsp; To me, it's important&nbsp;
we have that transparency at a local level.&nbsp;

01:36:01.760 --> 01:36:09.040
The next part then I just want to just highlight&nbsp;
to you is around the Maternity Safety Support&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:09.040 --> 01:36:14.640
Programme.&nbsp; Work is continuing and there's more&nbsp;
detail in the appendix.&nbsp; There's more – work is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:14.640 --> 01:36:21.120
continuing to really align that into our other&nbsp;
improvement work that, of course, Tim and Hugh&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:21.840 --> 01:36:28.400
are leading.&nbsp; The digital maternity programme&nbsp;
is progressing, and I'm really pleased.&nbsp; I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:29.040 --> 01:36:32.800
Baroness Cumberlege is particularly pleased&nbsp;
that we're starting to really make progress.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:33.520 --> 01:36:38.480
Indeed, over 100 bids came in by&nbsp;
the 15th of November deadline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:38.480 --> 01:36:42.560
and we're currently going through&nbsp;
the process – evaluation process.&nbsp;

01:36:44.160 --> 01:36:51.200
With regard to neonatal, and neonates are just&nbsp;
as important part of our maternity services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:51.200 --> 01:36:55.040
it's been part of our long-term plan&nbsp;
to enhance the experience of families&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:55.040 --> 01:37:01.840
and to increase the expert neonatal work.&nbsp; And&nbsp;
additional capital funding will be required&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:02.880 --> 01:37:07.680
to provide those additional cots and&nbsp;
the reconfiguration of services and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:07.680 --> 01:37:12.720
we're working with Julian and the finance&nbsp;
team to build this into our future plans.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:14.720 --> 01:37:22.480
Continuity of care is the right policy for&nbsp;
safety of women and their families.&nbsp; But what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:22.480 --> 01:37:30.160
we have done is listened to concerns raised by the&nbsp;
Royal College of Midwives, by heads of midwifery&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:30.160 --> 01:37:35.280
and others about this implementation.&nbsp;&nbsp;
So Professor Jacqueline Dunkley-Bent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:35.280 --> 01:37:42.480
in conjunction with other midwifery leaders&nbsp;
published a new continuity of care guidance&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:43.120 --> 01:37:49.600
recently that puts into place – articulates, puts&nbsp;
into place the building blocks to ensure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:49.600 --> 01:37:56.080
continuity of care can be delivered safely.
So finally, the next steps.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:58.720 --> 01:38:05.680
I would like to ask your permission that&nbsp;
our trust chairs and chief execs review&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:05.680 --> 01:38:10.960
their progress against the Ockenden&nbsp;
immediate and essential actions.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:11.600 --> 01:38:19.280
We will continue to align the Maternity Safety&nbsp;
Support Programme with our other system oversight&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:20.000 --> 01:38:26.560
work.&nbsp; The leadership development is going to be&nbsp;
really important that we continue.&nbsp; And we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:26.560 --> 01:38:33.520
work with Julian and the finance colleagues&nbsp;
to look at the ongoing investment of – into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:33.520 --> 01:38:40.320
maternity services for additional midwives and&nbsp;
obstetrician workforce, as well as for capital.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:41.360 --> 01:38:47.200
Chair, can I just quickly ask Jacqui Dunkley-Bent&nbsp;
just to talk about her equity strategy that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:47.200 --> 01:38:54.560
she published, and indeed, very quickly&nbsp;
Matthew Jolly to just talk about the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:54.560 --> 01:38:59.360
with the Royal College of Obstetricians.
&gt;&gt;Jacqueline Dunkley-Bent: Thank you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:59.360 --> 01:39:06.960
Ruth.&nbsp; So on the 6th of September this year,&nbsp;
we published the equity and equality guidance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:06.960 --> 01:39:12.640
and that guidance reflects five health inequality&nbsp;
priorities that are reflected in the planning&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:12.640 --> 01:39:19.840
guidance.&nbsp; And this guidance is really significant&nbsp;
in ensuring equity for everybody that uses our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:19.840 --> 01:39:26.800
maternity service, regardless of skin colour or&nbsp;
socioeconomic status.&nbsp; Now, the equity strategy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:27.520 --> 01:39:34.400
includes an action plan, aligns with the ICS&nbsp;
health inequalities work and indeed, is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:34.400 --> 01:39:40.720
of the core 20 plus five clinical priorities.&nbsp;&nbsp;
It includes the evidence-based interventions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:40.720 --> 01:39:46.400
resources, indicators, data sources, et cetera.&nbsp;&nbsp;
In addition to that guidance, we published four&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:46.400 --> 01:39:52.400
pledges for women and babies.&nbsp; And those four&nbsp;
pledges are really significant in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:52.960 --> 01:40:00.080
public confidence about our ambition to&nbsp;
reduce the equity gap.&nbsp; And the pledges are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:00.080 --> 01:40:04.480
integral to the documents so I won't read&nbsp;
through them in detail.&nbsp; But that's all I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:04.480 --> 01:40:10.800
say for now, Ruth, about the equity strategy.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Jacqui, is there anything else&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:10.800 --> 01:40:16.400
you wanted to say more broadly about midwifery?
&gt;&gt;Jacqueline Dunkley-Bent:&nbsp; Yes, I'm very happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:16.400 --> 01:40:23.520
to.&nbsp; Just in relation to the Ockenden assurance.&nbsp;&nbsp;
So we've been going through a very rigorous&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:24.080 --> 01:40:31.120
assurance process with our regional colleagues and&nbsp;
providers to ensure that the evidence submitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:31.680 --> 01:40:39.040
is validated and supporting services where&nbsp;
they aren't meeting those trajectories to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:39.040 --> 01:40:45.600
meet them at pace.&nbsp; Now, some of them are&nbsp;
restricted by the fact that we have staffing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:46.960 --> 01:40:54.320
challenges.&nbsp; So the recruitment benefits that Ruth&nbsp;
mentioned will be of benefit to the Ockenden.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:54.320 --> 01:41:01.760
Things like foetal monitoring assessment and other&nbsp;
items like that.&nbsp; So we are really relying on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:01.760 --> 01:41:08.480
recruitment to support services with some of the&nbsp;
Ockenden recommendations that they're not quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:08.480 --> 01:41:18.080
meeting at this point.&nbsp; We hope to have final&nbsp;
reports in terms of validated evidence against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:18.080 --> 01:41:24.880
immediate essential actions in the next few&nbsp;
weeks in preparation for the one-year anniversary&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:24.880 --> 01:41:29.520
of the Ockenden report.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:29.520 --> 01:41:34.240
Thanks, Jacqui.&nbsp; Is Matthew Jolly here as well?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Jolly: I am, Chair.&nbsp; Thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:34.240 --> 01:41:36.560
you very much.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Hi.&nbsp;

01:41:36.560 --> 01:41:42.080
&gt;&gt;Matthew Jolly: Hi.&nbsp; So just briefly to pick up&nbsp;
on the workforce and the work we're doing with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:42.080 --> 01:41:47.760
Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists&nbsp;
and also with Health Education England.&nbsp; It feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:47.760 --> 01:41:51.280
a very different place now.&nbsp; We've got a&nbsp;
really good collaboration going together.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:52.960 --> 01:41:58.880
The – one of the key document that has been recent&nbsp;
published is a document that we fed into the roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:58.880 --> 01:42:06.640
and responsibilities of the acute care consultant,&nbsp;
which not only starts to talk about the resourcing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:06.640 --> 01:42:11.520
for labour, but about behaviours and starting&nbsp;
to address some of the cultural leadership&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:12.080 --> 01:42:17.360
side of things as well.&nbsp; So I think that's&nbsp;
a really important marker we put down about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:17.360 --> 01:42:23.200
what we expect from our frontline services.
But we're also working with the RCOG to try and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:23.200 --> 01:42:29.840
establish what the right workforce is for our very&nbsp;
different sorts of units around the country.&nbsp; Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:29.840 --> 01:42:34.880
only in terms of just the numbers of people in the&nbsp;
labour ward, but also about how we resource the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:34.880 --> 01:42:42.480
leadership in that service and particularly the&nbsp;
role of the triumvirate, the head of midwifery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:42.480 --> 01:42:46.000
the clinical director and the lead manager.&nbsp;&nbsp;
And that's something that we're trying to mirror&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:47.280 --> 01:42:51.600
through the whole of the organisation.&nbsp; Whether&nbsp;
it's in terms of the labour ward leadership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:51.600 --> 01:42:57.280
the patient safety leadership the leadership&nbsp;
within trust, but also all the way through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:57.280 --> 01:43:01.920
LMNSs.&nbsp; And all the way up to the board&nbsp;
here today, which is why Jacqui and myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:02.720 --> 01:43:08.480
and having Andrew Morris as our national&nbsp;
safety champion is really important as well.&nbsp;

01:43:09.920 --> 01:43:14.240
So what we're hoping is that the&nbsp;
workforce tool that RCOG are developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:14.960 --> 01:43:22.240
will help us determine the right workforce for our&nbsp;
different sorts of units.&nbsp; As I say, not only to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:22.240 --> 01:43:27.600
deliver personalised and safe care, but to get the&nbsp;
leadership there so that we can affect the culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:27.600 --> 01:43:36.240
and improve outcomes and meet the ambition to&nbsp;
halve perinatal maternal deaths and to make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:36.240 --> 01:43:44.320
serious intent on those litigation costs as well.&nbsp;
So and I suppose a final thing to add to that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:44.320 --> 01:43:51.600
something that Amanda Pritchard pointed out when&nbsp;
she was in her previous role.&nbsp; We've struggled&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:54.000 --> 01:43:56.160
with the soft process in terms of how we keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:56.720 --> 01:44:02.160
tabs on maternity services and how well they're&nbsp;
performing.&nbsp; And I think we've got an opportunity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:02.160 --> 01:44:07.440
we're developing these workforce tools, to have a&nbsp;
way where we can develop some assurance about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:07.440 --> 01:44:12.080
well-performing our maternity services are by&nbsp;
looking at how well-resourced they are, making&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:12.080 --> 01:44:18.720
sure they're resourced for success.&nbsp; Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Matthew, thank you very much for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:18.720 --> 01:44:20.800
that.&nbsp; And Andrew Morris.
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:21.520 --> 01:44:27.600
Thanks, David.&nbsp; Just want to pay tribute to the&nbsp;
progress that's been made so far, particularly on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:27.600 --> 01:44:34.480
recruiting more midwives.&nbsp; It's been fantastic.&nbsp;&nbsp;
I suppose the bit that concerns me the most is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:34.480 --> 01:44:39.280
the pace at which we're proceeding around&nbsp;
consultant cover because consulting cover is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:39.280 --> 01:44:47.600
just so important around basic safety, seven days&nbsp;
a week on the [inaudible] suite.&nbsp; So any work that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:47.600 --> 01:44:53.600
you're doing currently, Matthew, with the Royal&nbsp;
College is really important to put some pace&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:53.600 --> 01:45:01.440
behind it and come up with a minimum standard for&nbsp;
a typical unit that delivers, I don't know, 3,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:02.000 --> 01:45:08.000
babies a year and another standard, so 5,000 or&nbsp;
6,000 deliveries because they are very different.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:08.560 --> 01:45:14.880
But it's really important that NHSE and the Royal&nbsp;
College develop the baseline that we expect all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:14.880 --> 01:45:20.800
providers to meet going forward.&nbsp; And I think this&nbsp;
is a really, really crucial aspect in improving&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:20.800 --> 01:45:26.160
the safety of our maternity units in England.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Jolly: Absolutely, Andrew. &nbsp;Thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:26.160 --> 01:45:29.680
you for making that point.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Can I just ask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:29.680 --> 01:45:34.720
this may be a very stupid question, Matthew,&nbsp;
you're not obstetrician and a gynaecologist.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:34.720 --> 01:45:39.034
Is that right?&nbsp; You are both.&nbsp; Is that correct?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Jolly: I was trained as both, and then I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:39.034 --> 01:45:44.800
subspecialised into foetal medicine.&nbsp; And I&nbsp;
dropped my gynaecology a little while ago so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:44.800 --> 01:45:49.360
I could do everything else.&nbsp; But that has created&nbsp;
challenges in my own unit because we do need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:50.240 --> 01:45:54.480
make sure that we can deal with those gynaecology&nbsp;
emergencies as they come through at night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:55.520 --> 01:46:02.960
the ruptured ectopics, et cetera.&nbsp; So we have now&nbsp;
a couple of absolute specialist gynaecologists&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:02.960 --> 01:46:07.520
who are on call when I'm on call so I can cover&nbsp;
the maternity emergencies, and they can cover the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:07.520 --> 01:46:13.520
gynae emergencies.&nbsp; So I think it's – there&nbsp;
is definitely a direction of travel of more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:13.520 --> 01:46:19.600
specialism in both obstetrics and gynaecology,&nbsp;
but we do have to think about how we maintain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:19.600 --> 01:46:29.200
our emergency gynaecology service in small units.&nbsp;&nbsp;
So I see there being more obstetric-only posts in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:29.200 --> 01:46:34.160
the future and more gynaecology-only posts in&nbsp;
the future, but there may be some areas where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:34.160 --> 01:46:38.960
we need to have those generalists.&nbsp; And I&nbsp;
know that that is something that the RCOG&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:38.960 --> 01:46:44.960
are thinking about in terms of what skillsets&nbsp;
our emergency gynaecologists require as well.&nbsp;

01:46:47.360 --> 01:46:53.600
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good.&nbsp; Thank you.&nbsp; David Behan.
&gt;&gt;David Behan: So thank you, Ruth and colleagues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:53.600 --> 01:46:58.800
for the progress and it's good to see the&nbsp;
progress.&nbsp; And can I just endorse the comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:58.800 --> 01:47:04.560
that were made about the joint work between Health&nbsp;
Education England both by Ruth and colleagues, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:04.560 --> 01:47:13.360
also by Claire and Tim earlier.&nbsp; But the question&nbsp;
I want to explore, Annexe A lists the trusts in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:13.360 --> 01:47:19.040
the Maternity Safety Support Programme.&nbsp; I'm&nbsp;
just trying to connect the actions and the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:19.040 --> 01:47:25.840
steps which are described in the report, and our&nbsp;
confidence and optimism about the speed at which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:25.840 --> 01:47:35.680
those 23 trusts are going to get off that list and&nbsp;
into a better place.&nbsp; Because I guess if I was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:35.680 --> 01:47:42.480
expectant family in any of those areas, my degree&nbsp;
of confidence might be very low.&nbsp; And I'm coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:42.480 --> 01:47:48.720
at this quite deliberately from the perspective&nbsp;
of people using services and the confidence we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:48.720 --> 01:47:53.040
take.&nbsp; So it's not to pick holes in what you've&nbsp;
said, Ruth, but I just think there's an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:54.240 --> 01:48:00.160
dimension to this, which is not just about&nbsp;
the workforce, et cetera, but it's about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:00.160 --> 01:48:06.480
people experience these services and just that&nbsp;
degree of confidence people need.&nbsp; And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:06.480 --> 01:48:11.760
what I'm interested in exploring.&nbsp; Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: David, thank you.&nbsp; So absolutely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:11.760 --> 01:48:18.880
HE colleagues have been part of this work, whether&nbsp;
it's with the future obstetrician workforce or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:18.880 --> 01:48:27.520
midwifery workforce.&nbsp; They are part of that.&nbsp; So&nbsp;
and I'm grateful for that one team.&nbsp; With regard&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:27.520 --> 01:48:34.960
to the maternity support programme, so it's really&nbsp;
important that we amended the criteria back some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:34.960 --> 01:48:42.880
time ago now to – not to be wait until the CQC&nbsp;
had given them a red rating, but to move into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:42.880 --> 01:48:48.080
'let's support people before they get to that'&nbsp;
and so moving much more upstream.&nbsp; And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:48.080 --> 01:48:52.480
there's a bit of further work that we're going to&nbsp;
need to do to make – to go even further upstream.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:53.200 --> 01:48:59.840
But Sascha Wells who works – Deputy Chief&nbsp;
Midwifery Officer who works with Jacqui&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:00.560 --> 01:49:07.280
and is part of the team and of course, she was&nbsp;
a Chief Midwife herself in a challenged unit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:08.560 --> 01:49:15.360
has been working with Sue Holden in Tim and&nbsp;
Hugh's team to make sure that we support people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:16.800 --> 01:49:23.760
move on in their journey in a positive way so that&nbsp;
they do actually continue to improve the outcomes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:23.760 --> 01:49:28.560
improve their relationships, much of the cultural&nbsp;
work.&nbsp; But one of the things we've just agreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:28.560 --> 01:49:38.720
is that, with the CQC we've agreed that we&nbsp;
will – new criteria for exiting this support&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:38.720 --> 01:49:45.360
programme that's ahead of when the CQC goes&nbsp;
in to do an inspection.&nbsp; So we will no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:45.360 --> 01:49:52.400
wait for CQC to do an inspection before we&nbsp;
remove them from this support programme.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:52.400 --> 01:49:58.240
We will do that with Tim's colleagues and&nbsp;
their support in conjunction with the CQC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:58.240 --> 01:50:05.200
and the CQC have been very supportive of that&nbsp;
proposal.&nbsp; Jacqui, what have I missed?&nbsp; Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:05.200 --> 01:50:09.520
there anything else you wanted to build on that?
&gt;&gt;Jacqueline Dunkley-Bent: Just to say that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:09.520 --> 01:50:16.080
have prioritised staffing numbers for the trusts&nbsp;
on the Maternity Safety Support Programme.&nbsp; We&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:16.080 --> 01:50:23.040
also have regional obstetricians working alongside&nbsp;
regional chief midwives to support providers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:23.040 --> 01:50:31.320
with the maternity improvement advisor. &nbsp;And also,&nbsp;
just lastly myself, Matthew Jolly, Sascha, Ross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:31.320 --> 01:50:37.680
Monroe, regional chief obstetricians and regional&nbsp;
colleagues are cantering around England speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:37.680 --> 01:50:46.400
to every exec team about maternity safety and&nbsp;
talking about reassurance and assurance and really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:46.400 --> 01:50:52.800
focusing on some of the self-assessment&nbsp;
items that will enable you to provide a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:52.800 --> 01:50:58.160
safe and sustainable maternity service.&nbsp; So&nbsp;
just a few things to add there.&nbsp; Thank you, Ruth&nbsp;

01:50:58.160 --> 01:51:01.360
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Jacqui.&nbsp; Matthew,&nbsp;
just one last question for you.&nbsp; I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:02.320 --> 01:51:10.400
if you had to rely on just three-ish predictive&nbsp;
indicators about the future safety of a maternity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:10.400 --> 01:51:16.480
unit, what would you focus on?&nbsp; What are the one&nbsp;
or two big things that you would focus to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:16.480 --> 01:51:21.040
to try and get a view as to whether or not the&nbsp;
maternity unit was going to be safe in the future?&nbsp;

01:51:21.040 --> 01:51:26.640
&gt;&gt;Matthew Jolly: The first one would be, are they&nbsp;
resourced properly in terms of workforce, without&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:26.640 --> 01:51:34.160
a doubt.&nbsp; Because the mistake we've made in the&nbsp;
past is we've looked at morbidity and mortality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:34.160 --> 01:51:38.640
which thankfully, are still actually really&nbsp;
very rare events in maternity.&nbsp; They're just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:38.640 --> 01:51:46.400
particularly tragic and awful events.&nbsp; But they&nbsp;
are so dependent on your case mix you serve – the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:46.400 --> 01:51:50.400
area you serve and all sorts of other things.&nbsp;&nbsp;
They don't – they're not sensitive enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:50.400 --> 01:51:53.680
and they have failed to pick up where&nbsp;
trusts have struggled in the past.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:53.680 --> 01:52:00.320
But what we know from the Maternity Safety Support&nbsp;
Programme is that the trusts that run into trouble&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:00.320 --> 01:52:06.000
are often the ones that are most poorly&nbsp;
resourced.&nbsp; So workforce would be my first one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:06.000 --> 01:52:11.040
I think I'd like to do something – ideally,&nbsp;
I'd develop some patient recorded experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:11.040 --> 01:52:14.640
measures and start to feel what it's&nbsp;
like for the women who use the service.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:14.640 --> 01:52:20.240
That's what – I think that would tell us a&nbsp;
lot more, and so that would be my second one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:21.440 --> 01:52:25.600
The third one I think would be something&nbsp;
to do with staff morale, probably.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:26.160 --> 01:52:30.000
I think then the clinical outcomes,&nbsp;
we will have dashboards and other ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:30.000 --> 01:52:35.680
of then seeing what the outcomes look like.&nbsp;
That's off the top of my head.&nbsp; Perhaps if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:35.680 --> 01:52:40.640
had another ten minutes, I might do something&nbsp;
more about clinical outcomes.&nbsp; But there are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:40.640 --> 01:52:46.880
as we've seen from the health select committee&nbsp;
work trying to performance-manage trusts on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:46.880 --> 01:52:51.040
things like caesarean section rates ends&nbsp;
up being an absolute disaster.&nbsp; People&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:51.680 --> 01:52:57.200
work hard to meet the rates rather than putting&nbsp;
women at the centre of our decision-making.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:57.200 --> 01:53:01.520
And what we need to do is make sure that not only&nbsp;
our service is safe, but that's personalised.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:02.720 --> 01:53:05.360
So I'm really keen that we&nbsp;
don't lose that approach.&nbsp;

01:53:06.400 --> 01:53:10.880
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you.&nbsp; Well, Matthew and&nbsp;
Jacqui and Ruth, thank you all three of you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:10.880 --> 01:53:14.800
and Aidan as well very much for the work you're&nbsp;
doing in this area.&nbsp; It's really important.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:14.800 --> 01:53:21.440
Thank you.&nbsp; Right, the last item is the public&nbsp;
participation assurance.&nbsp; I think it's just for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:21.440 --> 01:53:27.200
noting unless anyone's got a comment they want&nbsp;
to make.&nbsp; In which case, any other business?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:29.120 --> 01:53:34.096
No.&nbsp; Good.&nbsp; Thank you all very much.

